WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.916 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.916 --> 00:00:03.832 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.832 --> 00:00:07.185 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:07.185 --> 00:00:11.510 align:middle line:90%


00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:12.920 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: All right.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:17.840 align:middle line:84%
So last time, we were talking
about English plurals.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.270 align:middle line:84%
And we went through
this fairly fast

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:23.420 align:middle line:84%
so I wanted to talk
about it again a little

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:27.350 align:middle line:84%
bit just to make sure
we're all on the same page.

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:33.860 align:middle line:84%
The descriptive fact is that
English plurals, putting aside

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:36.860 align:middle line:84%
"children," and "oxen,"
and "fish," and "sheep,"

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:38.540 align:middle line:84%
and other things
like that, there's

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:42.380 align:middle line:84%
a general plural that's used
most of the time in English

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:44.660 align:middle line:84%
that we typically
spell with an S.

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.170 align:middle line:84%
But you've heard
me talk about how

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:47.420 align:middle line:90%
I feel about English spelling.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:52.040 align:middle line:90%


00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.010 align:middle line:84%
It's pronounced in at least
three different ways, sometimes

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:57.470 align:middle line:84%
as a "z," like in
"dogs," sometimes

00:00:57.470 --> 00:01:01.250 align:middle line:84%
as an "s," like in cats
or giraffes, and sometimes

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:04.280 align:middle line:84%
as a schwa followed by a
"z," like in "bridges,"

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.560 align:middle line:90%
or "brushes," or "messes."

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:10.970 align:middle line:84%
And so descriptively,
it's schwa "z"

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:12.590 align:middle line:90%
after what's called a strident.

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:14.930 align:middle line:84%
So the stridents are
a particular class

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:17.750 align:middle line:84%
of fricatives that have a
lot of high frequency noise.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:21.440 align:middle line:84%
They are "s," and
"sh," and also the

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:25.190 align:middle line:84%
affricates that end in
those fricatives, the "chu,"

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:30.990 align:middle line:84%
and the "juh," "tsuh"
for that matter.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:33.080 align:middle line:90%
Those are all stridents.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.945 align:middle line:84%
And so you get schwa
"z" after a strident,

00:01:35.945 --> 00:01:37.820 align:middle line:84%
and then if you're not
looking at a strident,

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:39.050 align:middle line:90%
it's just voicing.

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:42.770 align:middle line:84%
You get "s" if the sound before
the plural is not strident,

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:45.950 align:middle line:84%
is voiceless, and you
get "z" if it's voiced.

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:48.240 align:middle line:84%
And we had talked
about all that before.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.720 align:middle line:84%
And what I asked
you to do last time,

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:52.970 align:middle line:84%
and I just want to go through
this exercise again real

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:56.420 align:middle line:84%
quick just to make sure
we're all following it.

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:57.560 align:middle line:90%
Oh, I get to take this off.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:00.620 align:middle line:90%
I keep forgetting that.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:04.670 align:middle line:84%
Want to go through the
story again real quick.

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:07.700 align:middle line:84%
Forget for a second
about the strident thing.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:12.110 align:middle line:84%
We said there are at least
two imaginable stories.

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:14.483 align:middle line:84%
We're in a room full of
very imaginative people.

00:02:14.483 --> 00:02:16.400 align:middle line:84%
There are lots and lots
of imaginable stories,

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.140 align:middle line:84%
but let's concentrate
on two that

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:20.300 align:middle line:90%
seem particularly attractive.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:24.530 align:middle line:84%
One would be, the plural
suffix is underlyingly an "s,",

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:27.410 align:middle line:84%
and after a voiced
sound, it becomes a "z."

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:29.180 align:middle line:84%
So it starts off
as an "s,", that's

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:30.590 align:middle line:84%
the one you're
getting in "cats."

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:34.610 align:middle line:84%
And when you put the "s"
after the "g" in "dog,"

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:36.980 align:middle line:90%
it voices to a "z."

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:39.360 align:middle line:84%
And the other imaginable
story would be the opposite.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.690 align:middle line:84%
It would say the plural
is underlyingly a "z,"

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:45.350 align:middle line:84%
and after a voiceless
consonant it becomes an "s."

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:46.910 align:middle line:84%
So that would say
the "z" that you're

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:51.160 align:middle line:84%
getting in "dogs" is
the basic plural suffix.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:53.560 align:middle line:84%
And it's devoicing
in words like "cats"

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:56.830 align:middle line:84%
because it's preceded by
a voiceless consonant.

00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:59.560 align:middle line:84%
And we convinced ourselves
that one of these is--

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:01.480 align:middle line:84%
or I tried to convince
you, let me put it

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:04.900 align:middle line:84%
that way, that one of these is
more attractive than the other,

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:09.790 align:middle line:84%
because one of these
allows us to treat

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:13.870 align:middle line:84%
the sound-changing question
as a general property

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:17.380 align:middle line:84%
of English phonology,
rather than as something we

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:20.450 align:middle line:84%
must specifically state
about this morpheme.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:22.870 align:middle line:84%
So I'll just go
through this again.

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:27.580 align:middle line:84%
On a theory where
the suffix is an "s"

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:32.470 align:middle line:84%
and it voices after a voiced
sound, we ask ourselves,

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:34.810 align:middle line:84%
could we make that a
general fact about English?

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:37.570 align:middle line:84%
That is, is it generally
true that if you

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:40.060 align:middle line:84%
have an "s" at
the end of a word,

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:43.000 align:middle line:84%
you can't have a
voiced sound before it?

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.610 align:middle line:84%
Are there any English
words that end

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:49.630 align:middle line:84%
with an "s," and before the
"s" there's something voiced?

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:56.630 align:middle line:84%
Can anybody come up with
one, a word like that?

00:03:56.630 --> 00:03:58.550 align:middle line:84%
Do people see why
I'm looking for one?

00:03:58.550 --> 00:03:59.134 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Faith?

00:03:59.134 --> 00:04:00.672 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: What about "ribs"?

00:04:00.672 --> 00:04:02.130 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
So ribs, if I were

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:05.010 align:middle line:84%
going to write that
in IPA, would be that.

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:06.372 align:middle line:90%
That's a "z" sound.

00:04:06.372 --> 00:04:08.580 align:middle line:84%
So is there a word where
there's an "z" sound that's,

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:09.930 align:middle line:84%
right before it, there's
something voiced?

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:10.185 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:04:10.185 --> 00:04:11.140 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "Tense."

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:12.182 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "Tense."

00:04:12.182 --> 00:04:14.360 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's a good one.

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:16.300 align:middle line:90%
So that's "tense" in IPA.

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:18.850 align:middle line:84%
I guess if I'm
being more specific,

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:21.069 align:middle line:84%
there's an aspirated
"t" there, "tense."

00:04:21.069 --> 00:04:24.535 align:middle line:84%
OK, so that's one where that
could be the right theory,

00:04:24.535 --> 00:04:27.160 align:middle line:84%
but it would have to be a theory
that made a specific reference

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:29.800 align:middle line:84%
to this morpheme that said,
special thing about the plural

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:32.020 align:middle line:84%
morpheme, it voices
after a voiced sound.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:34.150 align:middle line:84%
Because there's nothing
about English phonology

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:36.910 align:middle line:84%
that prevents us
from having words

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:40.300 align:middle line:84%
that end in an "s" immediately
preceded by a voiced sound.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:42.670 align:middle line:84%
Suppose we do it the
other way around.

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:46.180 align:middle line:84%
If the plural is
underlyingly a "z" and then

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:49.150 align:middle line:84%
we say it devoices after
a voiceless consonant.

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:50.950 align:middle line:84%
Can we think of
any English words

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:52.900 align:middle line:84%
that end in a "z,"
and before the "z,"

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:55.810 align:middle line:90%
there's something voiceless?

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:57.442 align:middle line:90%
Sorry, you have another comment?

00:04:57.442 --> 00:04:59.650 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Are we not thinking
something like plushes,

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:01.673 align:middle line:84%
or something that's
like [INAUDIBLE]??

00:05:01.673 --> 00:05:03.340 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: OK,
so plushes, suppose

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:07.690 align:middle line:84%
I write that down,
"pluh," "shes."

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:11.350 align:middle line:84%
This is an instance of what I
asked you to not think about

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:15.160 align:middle line:84%
for a while, but you're
a rebel, I'm sorry.

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:18.070 align:middle line:84%
Here we've got the plural suffix
and it's after a strident,

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:20.228 align:middle line:84%
so we're getting
this schwa that's

00:05:20.228 --> 00:05:22.270 align:middle line:84%
put in here because there's
a strident before it.

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:25.220 align:middle line:84%
I started this by saying,
let's put that aside for now

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:29.720 align:middle line:84%
and we'll come back
to it, so good point.

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:31.790 align:middle line:84%
But actually, this isn't
an example of a word

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:33.770 align:middle line:84%
that ends in a Z and before
there's something voiceless

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:34.728 align:middle line:90%
because this is voiced.

00:05:34.728 --> 00:05:37.430 align:middle line:84%
So we're looking for a word that
ends in a Z, and before the Z,

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:41.130 align:middle line:84%
there's something voiceless,
sort of the opposite of tense.

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:43.570 align:middle line:90%
Are there any words like that?

00:05:43.570 --> 00:05:45.384 align:middle line:90%
I heard an "uh."

00:05:45.384 --> 00:05:46.680 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "Blitz"?

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:47.722 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Sorry?

00:05:47.722 --> 00:05:49.680 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I don't know,
I was thinking "blitz,"

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:50.810 align:middle line:90%
but that's [INAUDIBLE].

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:51.260 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "Blitz"?

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:51.760 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:54.110 align:middle line:84%
Well, so that's a word
that when we spell it,

00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:55.780 align:middle line:90%
we spell it like this.

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:57.530 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But it still
sounds like an "s."

00:05:57.530 --> 00:05:58.040 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:05:58.040 --> 00:05:58.880 align:middle line:90%
Right, exactly.

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:01.250 align:middle line:84%
When we pronounce
it, we pronounce

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:02.790 align:middle line:90%
it like that, I think, "blitz."

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:08.370 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, we spell it like that
because we got it from German.

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:09.812 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:06:09.812 --> 00:06:11.270 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:12.800 align:middle line:90%
So yeah, no.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:14.550 align:middle line:84%
There aren't any
English words like that.

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:18.980 align:middle line:90%
We don't have words like that.

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:20.720 align:middle line:84%
There aren't any
words like "cat-z."

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:23.540 align:middle line:84%
That's not a possible
English word.

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:27.320 align:middle line:84%
And so if we're willing to
say that the plural starts out

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:32.870 align:middle line:84%
as a "z," well, then
we can handle this part

00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:34.790 align:middle line:84%
of the-- not Raquel's
part, not the part

00:06:34.790 --> 00:06:37.362 align:middle line:84%
about "brushes" or
"plushes," where

00:06:37.362 --> 00:06:38.570 align:middle line:90%
there's a strident before it.

00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:40.340 align:middle line:84%
But putting the
stridents aside, we

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:44.850 align:middle line:84%
can handle this fact that the
plural suffix is sometimes

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:46.100 align:middle line:90%
a "z' and sometimes an "s."

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:48.798 align:middle line:84%
We can make that part of
English phonology generally.

00:06:48.798 --> 00:06:50.840 align:middle line:84%
We don't have to make any
statements specifically

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:54.710 align:middle line:84%
about this suffix,
which is pleasing.

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:57.740 align:middle line:84%
So maybe that's an argument
for making that move.

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:00.300 align:middle line:90%


00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:03.920 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, hypothesis two, the
plural is underlying a "z,"

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:06.018 align:middle line:84%
and it devoices after
voiceless consonants.

00:07:06.018 --> 00:07:08.060 align:middle line:84%
That can be part of a
general English fact, which

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:11.720 align:middle line:84%
is English doesn't have words
that end in a voiceless sound

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:12.970 align:middle line:90%
followed by a voiced sound.

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:16.100 align:middle line:90%


00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:19.760 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, after
a strident, we get

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:23.600 align:middle line:84%
schwa "z," "brushes,"
"messes," "latches," "mazes,"

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:26.810 align:middle line:84%
"plushes," that's
Raquel's example.

00:07:26.810 --> 00:07:31.700 align:middle line:84%
And English doesn't have words
that end in two stridents.

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:34.610 align:middle line:84%
So the plural of
"plush" is "plushez."

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:39.500 align:middle line:90%
It isn't "plushz."

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:43.470 align:middle line:84%
But we generally don't
have sequences like that.

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:45.290 align:middle line:84%
And actually, this
is an instance of we

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:48.080 align:middle line:84%
don't have "z" after
a voiceless sound.

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:51.350 align:middle line:90%
But we also don't have--

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:52.940 align:middle line:84%
yeah, what would
we be looking for?

00:07:52.940 --> 00:07:59.180 align:middle line:90%


00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:02.960 align:middle line:84%
I'm trying to think of
something that ends in "dz."

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:05.360 align:middle line:84%
Is this a real word, or is
it just something people use

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:06.235 align:middle line:90%
in crossword puzzles?

00:08:06.235 --> 00:08:08.780 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I think it's a tool.

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:11.450 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Tool,
like an ax, right?

00:08:11.450 --> 00:08:15.320 align:middle line:84%
So if I get to pronounce
this like this,

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:18.680 align:middle line:84%
the plural of it can't
be "adz-z," right?

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:21.620 align:middle line:90%
It's "adzez," I guess.

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:22.670 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:08:22.670 --> 00:08:25.037 align:middle line:84%
Actually, I don't know why
I went for obscure tools.

00:08:25.037 --> 00:08:27.620 align:middle line:84%
I could have done "mazes," which
we have up here on the board.

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:28.220 align:middle line:90%
OK, yeah.

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:28.700 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "Rose"?

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:28.955 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Sorry?

00:08:28.955 --> 00:08:29.780 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "Rose."

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:30.780 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "Rose."

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:34.039 align:middle line:84%
"Rose" would have
done even better, yes.

00:08:34.039 --> 00:08:35.030 align:middle line:90%
"Rose."

00:08:35.030 --> 00:08:37.400 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, plural is "roses."

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:42.110 align:middle line:84%
So English doesn't have words
that end in multiple stridents,

00:08:42.110 --> 00:08:44.480 align:middle line:84%
and so yeah, we're
putting a schwa there.

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:47.960 align:middle line:84%
Not something that we have
to state maybe specifically

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:50.180 align:middle line:84%
about this morpheme,
it's just a general fact

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:51.300 align:middle line:90%
about English phonology.

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:54.310 align:middle line:90%


00:08:54.310 --> 00:09:04.050 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, we can state rules that
say things like an obstruent--

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:06.550 align:middle line:84%
I'm sorry, I haven't introduced
you to the word "obstruent."

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:17.020 align:middle line:84%
Obstruent is a word that
covers stops and fricatives.

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:21.220 align:middle line:84%
Obstruents are sounds
that create a build up

00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:24.140 align:middle line:90%
of pressure in the oral cavity.

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:26.830 align:middle line:84%
So stops and fricatives
are obstruents.

00:09:26.830 --> 00:09:31.680 align:middle line:84%
Liquids, and glides, and
vowels, and what else, liquids,

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:35.280 align:middle line:84%
and glides, and vowels, and
nasals are not obstruents.

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:39.120 align:middle line:84%
So oral stops and fricatives,
those are obstruents.

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:44.980 align:middle line:84%
Obstruents become voiceless
after something voiceless.

00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:48.450 align:middle line:84%
So underlying "cat-z"
becomes "cats."

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:52.182 align:middle line:84%
And we have another rule
here that inserts a schwa,

00:09:52.182 --> 00:09:53.640 align:middle line:84%
and this is really
just an exercise

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:55.890 align:middle line:84%
in showing you what phonological
rules can look like.

00:09:55.890 --> 00:09:58.590 align:middle line:84%
That second rule inserts a
schwa between two stridents

00:09:58.590 --> 00:09:59.530 align:middle line:90%
at the end of a word.

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:02.130 align:middle line:84%
So "inserts a
schwa," the way you

00:10:02.130 --> 00:10:05.520 align:middle line:84%
say that with these rules is
"nothingness becomes a schwa."

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:09.660 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, I always liked that
way of describing insertion.

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:12.180 align:middle line:84%
So you have this place where
there isn't anything and it

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:16.050 align:middle line:84%
turns into something, namely
a schwa, between stridents,

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:17.940 align:middle line:90%
so "brush-z" becomes "brushez."

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:19.620 align:middle line:84%
So we can state
these rules, but we

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:22.620 align:middle line:84%
can think of these
rules as things

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:26.940 align:middle line:84%
that English does in order to
enforce its general conditions

00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:31.000 align:middle line:84%
on what words can look like
in these particular cases.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:33.813 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense so far?

00:10:33.813 --> 00:10:34.980 align:middle line:90%
Questions about any of that?

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:38.800 align:middle line:90%


00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:42.890 align:middle line:84%
Oh, yeah, and then,
if the only goal--

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:45.600 align:middle line:84%
sorry, I should have remembered
I was going to do this here.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:49.490 align:middle line:84%
If the only goal of
these sound changes

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:55.910 align:middle line:84%
is to create words that
obey general conditions

00:10:55.910 --> 00:11:00.560 align:middle line:84%
on English phonology, then
we might ask ourselves,

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:02.190 align:middle line:84%
suppose you start
off with that word.

00:11:02.190 --> 00:11:04.357 align:middle line:84%
What is that word I've got
down there at the bottom?

00:11:04.357 --> 00:11:08.898 align:middle line:90%


00:11:08.898 --> 00:11:10.440 align:middle line:84%
Forget about the
plural suffix on it.

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:11.700 align:middle line:90%
What's the singular?

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:12.450 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "Brush"?

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:13.090 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "Brush."

00:11:13.090 --> 00:11:13.590 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:17.850 align:middle line:84%
So suppose we start with "brush"
and we're going to add the "z."

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:20.580 align:middle line:84%
Why not change
the "z" to an "s"?

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:23.400 align:middle line:84%
After all, it's right next to
a voiceless sound, giving you

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:25.260 align:middle line:90%
"brushs."

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:29.610 align:middle line:84%
And then they insert a
schwa, giving you "brushes."

00:11:29.610 --> 00:11:30.735 align:middle line:90%
That's not what you do.

00:11:30.735 --> 00:11:33.630 align:middle line:90%


00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:36.540 align:middle line:84%
And we could wonder,
why not, particularly

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:39.720 align:middle line:84%
if I've posited the two rules
that I've got here, right?

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:43.410 align:middle line:84%
So I've got a rule that
is going to devoice

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:45.270 align:middle line:84%
the "z" if it's at
the end of a word

00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:47.490 align:middle line:90%
and after something voiceless.

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:50.820 align:middle line:84%
And I've got a rule that
inserts schwa between stridents.

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:53.340 align:middle line:84%
If I applied that first rule
first and then the second rule,

00:11:53.340 --> 00:11:55.230 align:middle line:90%
I would get this consequence.

00:11:55.230 --> 00:11:57.895 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:11:57.895 --> 00:11:59.770 align:middle line:84%
And there are at least
two ways of thinking--

00:11:59.770 --> 00:12:01.290 align:middle line:84%
sorry, you're
making hand gestures

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:03.220 align:middle line:84%
that suggest you can see
where I'm going with this--

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:04.650 align:middle line:84%
There are at least two ways
of thinking about this.

00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:06.670 align:middle line:84%
There's the mechanical
way, which is to say, well,

00:12:06.670 --> 00:12:08.462 align:middle line:84%
we're learning something
about these rules.

00:12:08.462 --> 00:12:09.754 align:middle line:90%
They don't apply in that order.

00:12:09.754 --> 00:12:11.962 align:middle line:84%
You don't do the first rule
and then the second rule.

00:12:11.962 --> 00:12:13.980 align:middle line:84%
You do the second rule
and then the first rule.

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:17.740 align:middle line:84%
That's one way to
think about this.

00:12:17.740 --> 00:12:20.610 align:middle line:84%
There's another way to
think about it, though.

00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:22.370 align:middle line:84%
Look, what we've been
saying right along

00:12:22.370 --> 00:12:26.690 align:middle line:84%
is these rules are things
that English does in order

00:12:26.690 --> 00:12:31.430 align:middle line:84%
to make these plurals obey
general English rules about how

00:12:31.430 --> 00:12:32.870 align:middle line:90%
sound can be put together.

00:12:32.870 --> 00:12:38.400 align:middle line:84%
I'm waving my arms a lot because
I'm avoiding a technical term.

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:41.446 align:middle line:84%
And that's dumb, so let me just
teach you a technical term.

00:12:41.446 --> 00:12:47.000 align:middle line:84%
Phonotactics, this
is the conditions

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:51.740 align:middle line:84%
on how sounds can combine, so
what the rules are for which

00:12:51.740 --> 00:12:54.470 align:middle line:90%
sounds can be where in a word.

00:12:54.470 --> 00:12:57.350 align:middle line:84%
And I've now several times in
the course of this lecture,

00:12:57.350 --> 00:12:59.060 align:middle line:84%
I've almost said the
word "phonotactics,"

00:12:59.060 --> 00:13:00.170 align:middle line:84%
and then stopped
and said, wait, I

00:13:00.170 --> 00:13:01.460 align:middle line:84%
haven't taught
them that word yet.

00:13:01.460 --> 00:13:03.210 align:middle line:84%
So I've been saying
things like "the rules

00:13:03.210 --> 00:13:05.030 align:middle line:90%
for how words can combine."

00:13:05.030 --> 00:13:08.780 align:middle line:84%
So now you know that
word, too, "phonotactics."

00:13:08.780 --> 00:13:10.370 align:middle line:84%
Rules of English
phonotactics include

00:13:10.370 --> 00:13:17.990 align:middle line:84%
things like "English words can't
end in a voiced sound that's

00:13:17.990 --> 00:13:20.000 align:middle line:84%
immediately preceded
by a voiceless sound,"

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:24.530 align:middle line:84%
or "English words can't end
in two stridents in a row."

00:13:24.530 --> 00:13:27.260 align:middle line:84%
So we have these
general conditions

00:13:27.260 --> 00:13:29.360 align:middle line:84%
on English phonotactics,
these conditions

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:30.620 align:middle line:90%
on how sounds can combine.

00:13:30.620 --> 00:13:33.170 align:middle line:84%
And those two rules,
those two things

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:35.330 align:middle line:84%
that happen to
sequences of sounds

00:13:35.330 --> 00:13:38.690 align:middle line:84%
are ways of enforcing
those general conditions

00:13:38.690 --> 00:13:40.100 align:middle line:90%
on phonotactics.

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:43.200 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:46.070 align:middle line:84%
If we think about it that
way, that is, if we think,

00:13:46.070 --> 00:13:49.397 align:middle line:84%
there is, on the one
hand, the rules--

00:13:49.397 --> 00:13:51.480 align:middle line:84%
the descriptions of the
things that are happening.

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:53.790 align:middle line:84%
And then there is,
on the other hand,

00:13:53.790 --> 00:13:56.750 align:middle line:84%
the reasons why these
things are happening.

00:13:56.750 --> 00:13:58.370 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:13:58.370 --> 00:14:00.830 align:middle line:84%
So the reasons why these
things are happening

00:14:00.830 --> 00:14:03.560 align:middle line:84%
are things like, English
doesn't like two stridents

00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:05.030 align:middle line:90%
at the end of a word.

00:14:05.030 --> 00:14:07.250 align:middle line:84%
That's why that second
rule is happening.

00:14:07.250 --> 00:14:09.440 align:middle line:84%
That's what it's for,
it's to stop that

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:12.233 align:middle line:84%
from happening in the case
of the English plural.

00:14:12.233 --> 00:14:14.150 align:middle line:84%
So we were going to draw
this distinction then

00:14:14.150 --> 00:14:15.920 align:middle line:84%
between phonotactics,
which say there

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:18.210 align:middle line:84%
are some combinations
of sounds that are bad,

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:20.480 align:middle line:84%
and things like these
rules which say,

00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:22.460 align:middle line:84%
and if you have these
combinations of sounds,

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:25.890 align:middle line:84%
here's what you ought to
do to fix the problem.

00:14:25.890 --> 00:14:29.570 align:middle line:84%
If you think about it,
if I just tell you,

00:14:29.570 --> 00:14:33.020 align:middle line:84%
having a word that ends in two
stridents is bad in English.

00:14:33.020 --> 00:14:36.930 align:middle line:90%
So if I start off with "brush"--

00:14:36.930 --> 00:14:37.430 align:middle line:90%
oh, dear.

00:14:37.430 --> 00:14:39.110 align:middle line:84%
Now I have to write
an "r" upside down.

00:14:39.110 --> 00:14:39.980 align:middle line:90%
That's always hard.

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:42.500 align:middle line:90%


00:14:42.500 --> 00:14:44.060 align:middle line:84%
If I start off with
"brush" and we're

00:14:44.060 --> 00:14:47.090 align:middle line:84%
saying that the
plural is underlyingly

00:14:47.090 --> 00:14:51.230 align:middle line:84%
a "z," what we in fact do
is to introduce a schwa.

00:14:51.230 --> 00:14:54.830 align:middle line:90%


00:14:54.830 --> 00:14:56.952 align:middle line:84%
And we think the reason
that we're doing that is,

00:14:56.952 --> 00:14:58.910 align:middle line:84%
well, we're going to make
reference to the fact

00:14:58.910 --> 00:15:00.980 align:middle line:84%
that English generally
doesn't allow words

00:15:00.980 --> 00:15:02.480 align:middle line:90%
to end in multiple stridents.

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:04.730 align:middle line:84%
But if our only goal is
to make sure this doesn't

00:15:04.730 --> 00:15:06.860 align:middle line:84%
end in multiple
stridents, well, there

00:15:06.860 --> 00:15:10.340 align:middle line:84%
are lots of ways we
could fix that problem.

00:15:10.340 --> 00:15:13.250 align:middle line:90%
We could get rid of a strident.

00:15:13.250 --> 00:15:18.890 align:middle line:84%
The plural of "brush" could
be "bruhz," or "brush."

00:15:18.890 --> 00:15:22.160 align:middle line:84%
We could introduce something
other than a schwa.

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:23.720 align:middle line:84%
We could introduce
a different vowel.

00:15:23.720 --> 00:15:25.520 align:middle line:90%
It could be "brush-eez."

00:15:25.520 --> 00:15:27.110 align:middle line:90%
We could introduce a consonant.

00:15:27.110 --> 00:15:28.730 align:middle line:90%
It could be "brushts."

00:15:28.730 --> 00:15:30.620 align:middle line:84%
Actually, that's not
so easy to say either.

00:15:30.620 --> 00:15:32.030 align:middle line:90%
"Brush-dz."

00:15:32.030 --> 00:15:32.600 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:15:32.600 --> 00:15:34.475 align:middle line:84%
We could introduce some
other consonants that

00:15:34.475 --> 00:15:35.978 align:middle line:90%
would work better than these.

00:15:35.978 --> 00:15:38.270 align:middle line:84%
Heck, we could introduce lots
of consonants and vowels.

00:15:38.270 --> 00:15:44.840 align:middle line:84%
The plural of "brush"
could be "brush-glors."

00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:47.090 align:middle line:84%
You can just introduce a
whole chunk of stuff in here.

00:15:47.090 --> 00:15:49.640 align:middle line:84%
There's all kinds of stuff you
could do to fix that problem.

00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:53.300 align:middle line:90%
This is what you, in fact, do.

00:15:53.300 --> 00:15:56.810 align:middle line:84%
So when we say
we're going to cover

00:15:56.810 --> 00:15:58.970 align:middle line:84%
these facts about the
different allomorphs

00:15:58.970 --> 00:16:02.330 align:middle line:84%
of the English plural with
general English phonology,

00:16:02.330 --> 00:16:05.160 align:middle line:84%
is what I said, we got
ourselves halfway there.

00:16:05.160 --> 00:16:07.190 align:middle line:84%
We came up with
these generalizations

00:16:07.190 --> 00:16:08.882 align:middle line:90%
about English phonotactics.

00:16:08.882 --> 00:16:11.090 align:middle line:84%
English doesn't like words
that end in two stridents,

00:16:11.090 --> 00:16:11.760 align:middle line:90%
let's say.

00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:14.480 align:middle line:84%
And then we also have to have
this solution to the problem,

00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:16.640 align:middle line:90%
insert a schwa.

00:16:16.640 --> 00:16:19.850 align:middle line:84%
The account of what's
happening in English plurals

00:16:19.850 --> 00:16:21.590 align:middle line:90%
needs both of those components.

00:16:21.590 --> 00:16:23.930 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:16:23.930 --> 00:16:27.620 align:middle line:84%
Some of you are looking at me
as though I'm making sense.

00:16:27.620 --> 00:16:29.730 align:middle line:84%
It's always hard because
you're all wearing masks.

00:16:29.730 --> 00:16:31.220 align:middle line:84%
So I appreciate it,
you've spent all this time

00:16:31.220 --> 00:16:33.080 align:middle line:84%
working out ways of
communicating emotions

00:16:33.080 --> 00:16:34.892 align:middle line:84%
just with your eyebrows,
which is great.

00:16:34.892 --> 00:16:36.350 align:middle line:84%
All of you are
waving your eyebrows

00:16:36.350 --> 00:16:38.070 align:middle line:84%
in a semaphore-like
fashion at me.

00:16:38.070 --> 00:16:40.520 align:middle line:90%
I appreciate it a lot.

00:16:40.520 --> 00:16:42.570 align:middle line:84%
So getting back
to this question,

00:16:42.570 --> 00:16:49.610 align:middle line:84%
why not first devoice the "s,"
and then introduce the schwa?

00:16:49.610 --> 00:16:53.910 align:middle line:84%
The answer to that might
be, well, that first--

00:16:53.910 --> 00:16:56.490 align:middle line:84%
so I said, there are two ways
of talking about the answer

00:16:56.490 --> 00:16:56.990 align:middle line:90%
to that.

00:16:56.990 --> 00:16:59.198 align:middle line:84%
One is the mechanical way,
which says, no, that's not

00:16:59.198 --> 00:17:00.920 align:middle line:90%
the order these rules apply in.

00:17:00.920 --> 00:17:03.020 align:middle line:84%
That's the way we had
talked about this before.

00:17:03.020 --> 00:17:04.940 align:middle line:84%
We went through
these Lardil cases,

00:17:04.940 --> 00:17:08.420 align:middle line:84%
and I said, sure looks as though
we need to be willing to say,

00:17:08.420 --> 00:17:10.670 align:middle line:84%
there are all these things
that happen to these words,

00:17:10.670 --> 00:17:12.680 align:middle line:84%
but we need them to
happen in order so

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:14.359 align:middle line:90%
that we get the right answers.

00:17:14.359 --> 00:17:16.384 align:middle line:90%
And that works.

00:17:16.384 --> 00:17:19.062 align:middle line:90%


00:17:19.062 --> 00:17:21.020 align:middle line:84%
And in this particular
case, we could say that.

00:17:21.020 --> 00:17:21.812 align:middle line:90%
It would also work.

00:17:21.812 --> 00:17:23.750 align:middle line:84%
We could say, the
reason you don't

00:17:23.750 --> 00:17:26.250 align:middle line:84%
do what I've got up there on
the board, the reason you don't

00:17:26.250 --> 00:17:28.369 align:middle line:84%
first devoice the "z" and
then introduce the schwa

00:17:28.369 --> 00:17:31.190 align:middle line:84%
is that that's not the order
the operations take place in.

00:17:31.190 --> 00:17:33.770 align:middle line:84%
But maybe this way of talking
in terms of phonotactics

00:17:33.770 --> 00:17:36.980 align:middle line:90%
is also helpful here.

00:17:36.980 --> 00:17:39.470 align:middle line:84%
So we could say to
ourselves, the reason

00:17:39.470 --> 00:17:42.920 align:middle line:84%
that you don't first do
that first thing is that it

00:17:42.920 --> 00:17:46.100 align:middle line:90%
doesn't solve all the problems.

00:17:46.100 --> 00:17:49.310 align:middle line:84%
Inserting a schwa solves
all the problems at once.

00:17:49.310 --> 00:17:53.150 align:middle line:84%
By inserting a schwa,
you now no longer

00:17:53.150 --> 00:17:54.680 align:middle line:84%
have a word ending
in two stridents,

00:17:54.680 --> 00:17:58.820 align:middle line:84%
and you also no
longer have a word

00:17:58.820 --> 00:18:01.490 align:middle line:84%
ending in a voiced sound
preceded by a voiceless sound.

00:18:01.490 --> 00:18:03.860 align:middle line:84%
Because the "z" at the end
is now preceded by a schwa,

00:18:03.860 --> 00:18:05.880 align:middle line:90%
which is voiced.

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:08.940 align:middle line:84%
So maybe there's some kind of
principle of minimal repair,

00:18:08.940 --> 00:18:12.390 align:middle line:84%
maybe, that says, look around at
the various things you can do.

00:18:12.390 --> 00:18:14.420 align:middle line:90%
Do the most effective one.

00:18:14.420 --> 00:18:17.010 align:middle line:84%
That could be a way
to talk about this.

00:18:17.010 --> 00:18:20.600 align:middle line:84%
We're going to need to look at
more cases to try to decide.

00:18:20.600 --> 00:18:24.020 align:middle line:84%
But the point of going
through the English plural

00:18:24.020 --> 00:18:26.900 align:middle line:84%
in this level of detail was
to introduce you to this idea.

00:18:26.900 --> 00:18:28.790 align:middle line:84%
And having introduced
you to this idea,

00:18:28.790 --> 00:18:31.610 align:middle line:84%
we're now going to spend some
time with this idea, this idea

00:18:31.610 --> 00:18:33.860 align:middle line:84%
that when we're looking
at a phonological problem,

00:18:33.860 --> 00:18:36.830 align:middle line:84%
it's useful to think about
it in these two halves.

00:18:36.830 --> 00:18:40.460 align:middle line:84%
There's, on the one
hand, what's the problem?

00:18:40.460 --> 00:18:42.560 align:middle line:90%
Why are sounds changing?

00:18:42.560 --> 00:18:44.750 align:middle line:84%
And on the other hand,
what are the sounds doing?

00:18:44.750 --> 00:18:45.770 align:middle line:90%
What's the fix?

00:18:45.770 --> 00:18:47.070 align:middle line:90%
What's the repair?

00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:49.310 align:middle line:84%
And we want to think about
both of those things.

00:18:49.310 --> 00:18:50.640 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:52.830 align:middle line:84%
So in English, we've talked
about a couple of kinds

00:18:52.830 --> 00:18:54.780 align:middle line:84%
of problems that English
plurals can create,

00:18:54.780 --> 00:18:57.460 align:middle line:84%
and now we're also talking
about some specific repairs.

00:18:57.460 --> 00:18:59.520 align:middle line:84%
And we raise questions
for ourselves.

00:18:59.520 --> 00:19:00.420 align:middle line:90%
Why those repairs?

00:19:00.420 --> 00:19:02.725 align:middle line:90%
Why not other repairs?

00:19:02.725 --> 00:19:03.975 align:middle line:90%
We'll come back to that issue.

00:19:03.975 --> 00:19:08.230 align:middle line:90%


00:19:08.230 --> 00:19:11.710 align:middle line:84%
One consequence of
all this is that we're

00:19:11.710 --> 00:19:15.190 align:middle line:84%
able to apply the rules of
English plural formation

00:19:15.190 --> 00:19:18.970 align:middle line:84%
to words that don't have the
phonology of English words.

00:19:18.970 --> 00:19:21.430 align:middle line:84%
Here are two words
from other languages.

00:19:21.430 --> 00:19:24.300 align:middle line:84%
Anybody want to
pronounce them for us?

00:19:24.300 --> 00:19:24.800 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:26.008 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "Bach" and "rouge."

00:19:26.008 --> 00:19:28.280 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah,
"Bach" and "rouge."

00:19:28.280 --> 00:19:28.850 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:19:28.850 --> 00:19:32.990 align:middle line:84%
So where "Bach," if I'm
being unbearably pretentious

00:19:32.990 --> 00:19:36.020 align:middle line:84%
and I want to demonstrate for
you the fact that I speak some

00:19:36.020 --> 00:19:40.490 align:middle line:84%
German, I might pronounce the
name of the great German family

00:19:40.490 --> 00:19:45.230 align:middle line:84%
of German composers, the
Bachs, was a big family,

00:19:45.230 --> 00:19:47.705 align:middle line:84%
generations of Bachs
writing all kinds of things.

00:19:47.705 --> 00:19:50.270 align:middle line:90%


00:19:50.270 --> 00:19:52.130 align:middle line:84%
I might pronounce it
with a velar fricative.

00:19:52.130 --> 00:19:53.797 align:middle line:84%
The standard English
way to pronounce it

00:19:53.797 --> 00:19:56.297 align:middle line:84%
is with a velar stop because
we don't have velar fricatives.

00:19:56.297 --> 00:19:57.890 align:middle line:84%
But if I feel like
being pretentious,

00:19:57.890 --> 00:20:01.370 align:middle line:84%
I'll pronounce it with a
velar fricative, "Bach."

00:20:01.370 --> 00:20:06.070 align:middle line:84%
And then I've already done
this, but what's the plural?

00:20:06.070 --> 00:20:07.400 align:middle line:90%
"Bachs," right?

00:20:07.400 --> 00:20:09.670 align:middle line:90%
It's with an "s."

00:20:09.670 --> 00:20:10.810 align:middle line:90%
Why is it with an "s"?

00:20:10.810 --> 00:20:15.790 align:middle line:84%
Well, because the repairs
that we've been talking about

00:20:15.790 --> 00:20:18.590 align:middle line:84%
are repairs for
specific problems.

00:20:18.590 --> 00:20:24.400 align:middle line:84%
So by hypothesis, the plural
of this starts off as this.

00:20:24.400 --> 00:20:28.060 align:middle line:84%
And now you have a word that
ends in a voiced sound preceded

00:20:28.060 --> 00:20:29.300 align:middle line:90%
by a voiceless sound.

00:20:29.300 --> 00:20:32.740 align:middle line:84%
And the standard English
repair for that is to devoice,

00:20:32.740 --> 00:20:34.540 align:middle line:90%
so we change this "z" to an "s."

00:20:34.540 --> 00:20:35.665 align:middle line:90%
I get "Bachs."

00:20:35.665 --> 00:20:38.290 align:middle line:90%


00:20:38.290 --> 00:20:42.910 align:middle line:84%
Shows us that what we've got
in our heads is not just--

00:20:42.910 --> 00:20:45.688 align:middle line:84%
so we've been going around
this fact for a while now.

00:20:45.688 --> 00:20:47.230 align:middle line:84%
What we have in our
heads is not just

00:20:47.230 --> 00:20:53.170 align:middle line:84%
a list of English nouns in
their singular and plural forms.

00:20:53.170 --> 00:20:57.220 align:middle line:84%
What we have are rules,
algorithms for making plurals.

00:20:57.220 --> 00:20:58.720 align:middle line:84%
And you can apply
those algorithms

00:20:58.720 --> 00:21:00.897 align:middle line:84%
to words that are not
English words that

00:21:00.897 --> 00:21:02.980 align:middle line:84%
have sounds in them that
we don't have in English,

00:21:02.980 --> 00:21:07.120 align:middle line:84%
like "Bach," or "rouge," which
is the French word for "red,"

00:21:07.120 --> 00:21:09.700 align:middle line:84%
but I guess has been borrowed
into English as a name

00:21:09.700 --> 00:21:11.230 align:middle line:90%
for a component of makeup.

00:21:11.230 --> 00:21:14.530 align:middle line:84%
If you're talking about a bunch
of different kinds of rouge,

00:21:14.530 --> 00:21:17.110 align:middle line:84%
which of these various kinds
of rouge do you like best?

00:21:17.110 --> 00:21:20.462 align:middle line:90%
Well, I like those.

00:21:20.462 --> 00:21:21.670 align:middle line:90%
What's the plural of "rouge"?

00:21:21.670 --> 00:21:24.560 align:middle line:90%


00:21:24.560 --> 00:21:25.810 align:middle line:90%
"Rouges" [with schwa and "z"].

00:21:25.810 --> 00:21:28.170 align:middle line:90%
"I like those rouges over there.

00:21:28.170 --> 00:21:32.140 align:middle line:84%
I like these lipsticks
better than those rouges."

00:21:32.140 --> 00:21:33.230 align:middle line:90%
What did I do here?

00:21:33.230 --> 00:21:34.210 align:middle line:90%
I inserted a schwa.

00:21:34.210 --> 00:21:34.900 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:21:34.900 --> 00:21:37.863 align:middle line:84%
Well, because this
is a strident there.

00:21:37.863 --> 00:21:40.030 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't matter that it's
not an English strident.

00:21:40.030 --> 00:21:42.670 align:middle line:84%
We don't have that, except
in words that we borrowed

00:21:42.670 --> 00:21:45.000 align:middle line:90%
from French, like this one.

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:45.818 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:21:45.818 --> 00:21:47.490 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:21:47.490 --> 00:21:48.000 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:51.510 align:middle line:90%


00:21:51.510 --> 00:21:54.660 align:middle line:84%
So this has all been an attempt
to-- whoops, I spelled the "r"

00:21:54.660 --> 00:21:56.832 align:middle line:90%
right side up again.

00:21:56.832 --> 00:21:58.290 align:middle line:84%
This has all been
an attempt to get

00:21:58.290 --> 00:22:03.443 align:middle line:84%
you to take seriously the idea
that it's useful when you're

00:22:03.443 --> 00:22:04.860 align:middle line:84%
looking at a
phonological problem,

00:22:04.860 --> 00:22:07.620 align:middle line:84%
a phonological process,
to ask yourself both,

00:22:07.620 --> 00:22:09.480 align:middle line:90%
what is happening?

00:22:09.480 --> 00:22:10.800 align:middle line:90%
So what has changed?

00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:14.400 align:middle line:84%
What were the original
things that I was combining?

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:16.770 align:middle line:84%
Let's say if I was adding
an affix to a word,

00:22:16.770 --> 00:22:18.480 align:middle line:90%
and what has happened to them?

00:22:18.480 --> 00:22:19.860 align:middle line:90%
What form do they have now?

00:22:19.860 --> 00:22:20.940 align:middle line:90%
Ask yourself that.

00:22:20.940 --> 00:22:23.070 align:middle line:90%
And also ask, why?

00:22:23.070 --> 00:22:25.530 align:middle line:84%
So what general
properties of the language

00:22:25.530 --> 00:22:28.350 align:middle line:84%
are we trying to enforce
with this sound change?

00:22:28.350 --> 00:22:29.583 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:22:29.583 --> 00:22:31.000 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was
thinking about how

00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:33.250 align:middle line:84%
you were saying that certain
sound combinations just

00:22:33.250 --> 00:22:34.340 align:middle line:90%
don't sound right.

00:22:34.340 --> 00:22:37.120 align:middle line:84%
And I was thinking about how
if you didn't know what part

00:22:37.120 --> 00:22:41.402 align:middle line:84%
of speech the word "pore" is,
then if you were like, OK,

00:22:41.402 --> 00:22:43.860 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to pluralize it, then
it would sound weird to say

00:22:43.860 --> 00:22:44.550 align:middle line:90%
"por-s."

00:22:44.550 --> 00:22:46.050 align:middle line:84%
But then if you
knew it was supposed

00:22:46.050 --> 00:22:48.240 align:middle line:84%
to be an adjective, then
"porous" would actually

00:22:48.240 --> 00:22:50.520 align:middle line:90%
sound kind of fine.

00:22:50.520 --> 00:22:55.820 align:middle line:84%
So maybe there's some
element of more structure

00:22:55.820 --> 00:22:58.840 align:middle line:84%
that needs to be known to define
what sounds good or weird?

00:22:58.840 --> 00:23:01.567 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I think
I might see what you mean.

00:23:01.567 --> 00:23:03.400 align:middle line:84%
What was the word that
you started off with?

00:23:03.400 --> 00:23:03.890 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: "Pore."

00:23:03.890 --> 00:23:04.890 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "Pore."

00:23:04.890 --> 00:23:08.610 align:middle line:90%
You mean that word?

00:23:08.610 --> 00:23:09.110 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:23:09.110 --> 00:23:12.410 align:middle line:84%
And so we have those in our
skin, little holes in our skin.

00:23:12.410 --> 00:23:15.290 align:middle line:90%
And the plural is that, right?

00:23:15.290 --> 00:23:16.550 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:23:16.550 --> 00:23:17.310 align:middle line:90%
Oh, I see.

00:23:17.310 --> 00:23:26.057 align:middle line:84%
But you're saying-- so yeah,
there's an adjective, "porous."

00:23:26.057 --> 00:23:27.390 align:middle line:90%
Is that what you're thinking of?

00:23:27.390 --> 00:23:28.040 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:23:28.040 --> 00:23:37.850 align:middle line:84%
And yes, so I'm trying to
think about whether I can think

00:23:37.850 --> 00:23:40.115 align:middle line:84%
of any adjectives
that end with "-orz."

00:23:40.115 --> 00:23:44.550 align:middle line:90%


00:23:44.550 --> 00:23:47.197 align:middle line:84%
I have "pores" on the brain,
and "s'mores," and "floors,"

00:23:47.197 --> 00:23:49.530 align:middle line:84%
though everything I'm thinking
of off the top of my head

00:23:49.530 --> 00:23:52.580 align:middle line:90%
is plural.

00:23:52.580 --> 00:23:59.900 align:middle line:84%
There are some generalizations
about phonological differences

00:23:59.900 --> 00:24:06.290 align:middle line:84%
sometimes between, occasionally,
under certain circumstances,

00:24:06.290 --> 00:24:09.720 align:middle line:84%
things of different
syntactic categories.

00:24:09.720 --> 00:24:12.170 align:middle line:84%
So there are languages
that draw distinctions

00:24:12.170 --> 00:24:16.070 align:middle line:84%
between, say, nouns and verbs,
or nouns and adjectives.

00:24:16.070 --> 00:24:18.860 align:middle line:84%
And nouns have a particular
phonological signature,

00:24:18.860 --> 00:24:20.990 align:middle line:84%
and adjectives have
a different one.

00:24:20.990 --> 00:24:24.170 align:middle line:84%
We'll actually get a chance to
talk about an example like that

00:24:24.170 --> 00:24:28.530 align:middle line:84%
when we get to Japanese,
which has something like that.

00:24:28.530 --> 00:24:32.150 align:middle line:90%
It's common, for example, for--

00:24:32.150 --> 00:24:32.850 align:middle line:90%
well, yeah.

00:24:32.850 --> 00:24:33.795 align:middle line:90%
I'll leave it there.

00:24:33.795 --> 00:24:36.170 align:middle line:84%
When we get to Japanese, we'll
see something a little bit

00:24:36.170 --> 00:24:39.150 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:24:39.150 --> 00:24:42.290 align:middle line:84%
And so maybe, getting back
to your original comment,

00:24:42.290 --> 00:24:46.455 align:middle line:84%
your original comment was, I'm
talking as though what makes

00:24:46.455 --> 00:24:48.080 align:middle line:84%
something look like
a good English word

00:24:48.080 --> 00:24:50.600 align:middle line:84%
is just going to be true
of all English words.

00:24:50.600 --> 00:24:53.027 align:middle line:84%
But maybe there are cases
where we have to ask,

00:24:53.027 --> 00:24:54.860 align:middle line:84%
does this look like a
good English adjective

00:24:54.860 --> 00:24:56.330 align:middle line:90%
or a good English verb?

00:24:56.330 --> 00:24:57.140 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:24:57.140 --> 00:25:00.230 align:middle line:84%
And people have found things
very vaguely like that,

00:25:00.230 --> 00:25:02.840 align:middle line:84%
and I'll talk more about
it when we get to Japanese.

00:25:02.840 --> 00:25:05.510 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, nice point.

00:25:05.510 --> 00:25:08.013 align:middle line:84%
Other points people wanted to
make before we do Yawelmani?

00:25:08.013 --> 00:25:08.555 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Joseph?

00:25:08.555 --> 00:25:10.763 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I'll ask you a
question about transcription

00:25:10.763 --> 00:25:11.630 align:middle line:90%
of "porous."

00:25:11.630 --> 00:25:16.370 align:middle line:84%
If you use the power,
would you use--

00:25:16.370 --> 00:25:23.150 align:middle line:84%
why would you use [INAUDIBLE]
backwards C shape?

00:25:23.150 --> 00:25:26.535 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh,
you would use this?

00:25:26.535 --> 00:25:28.160 align:middle line:84%
I think I would be
tempted to pronounce

00:25:28.160 --> 00:25:30.640 align:middle line:90%
that "pah," "ah," "pars."

00:25:30.640 --> 00:25:34.750 align:middle line:84%
That's an "ah"
sound, and so I guess

00:25:34.750 --> 00:25:36.910 align:middle line:84%
that's why I wanted
to spell it like this.

00:25:36.910 --> 00:25:40.180 align:middle line:84%
I thought you were about to
ask me why I'm not spelling it,

00:25:40.180 --> 00:25:44.860 align:middle line:84%
say, like this, "porz" which
I guess I could also do,

00:25:44.860 --> 00:25:49.507 align:middle line:90%
like indicate the aspiration.

00:25:49.507 --> 00:25:52.090 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to quickly erase this
before anybody has any worries

00:25:52.090 --> 00:25:53.155 align:middle line:90%
along any of these lines.

00:25:53.155 --> 00:25:57.160 align:middle line:90%


00:25:57.160 --> 00:26:00.700 align:middle line:84%
Oh, and this actually
brings me to something.

00:26:00.700 --> 00:26:04.240 align:middle line:84%
I was raised, for some reason,
to talk about decisions

00:26:04.240 --> 00:26:09.370 align:middle line:84%
like this, decisions about
whether to spell aspiration

00:26:09.370 --> 00:26:14.050 align:middle line:84%
on English voiceless
stops, to talk about that

00:26:14.050 --> 00:26:17.020 align:middle line:84%
as a choice between
what I was taught

00:26:17.020 --> 00:26:20.050 align:middle line:84%
to call a "loose" and a
"tight" transcription.

00:26:20.050 --> 00:26:22.450 align:middle line:84%
But there is a more general
term, which turns out

00:26:22.450 --> 00:26:23.740 align:middle line:90%
to be used a lot more.

00:26:23.740 --> 00:26:25.690 align:middle line:90%
I don't know who did this to me.

00:26:25.690 --> 00:26:28.480 align:middle line:84%
The terms that people
use more commonly

00:26:28.480 --> 00:26:30.400 align:middle line:90%
are "broad" and "narrow."

00:26:30.400 --> 00:26:33.203 align:middle line:84%
One of the TAs pointed this
out to me after last time.

00:26:33.203 --> 00:26:34.870 align:middle line:84%
He came up and said,
why are you calling

00:26:34.870 --> 00:26:35.983 align:middle line:90%
them "tight" and "loose"?

00:26:35.983 --> 00:26:37.150 align:middle line:90%
I'm like, because I'm weird.

00:26:37.150 --> 00:26:38.057 align:middle line:90%
I'm sorry.

00:26:38.057 --> 00:26:39.640 align:middle line:84%
So "broad" and
"narrow" is what you'll

00:26:39.640 --> 00:26:42.520 align:middle line:84%
see more often, if you
look in the literature.

00:26:42.520 --> 00:26:44.770 align:middle line:84%
So I'm glad you asked your
question because it allowed

00:26:44.770 --> 00:26:48.108 align:middle line:90%
me to come clean about this.

00:26:48.108 --> 00:26:49.650 align:middle line:84%
Other questions
about why I am weird?

00:26:49.650 --> 00:26:50.190 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:26:50.190 --> 00:26:52.440 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Which one is broad,
and which one is narrow?

00:26:52.440 --> 00:26:54.120 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I'm sorry.

00:26:54.120 --> 00:26:58.770 align:middle line:84%
Narrow is when you are trying
to represent every fact.

00:26:58.770 --> 00:27:01.860 align:middle line:84%
Broad is when you leave out
facts that are predictable.

00:27:01.860 --> 00:27:04.800 align:middle line:84%
So a broad
transcription of "tense"

00:27:04.800 --> 00:27:07.320 align:middle line:84%
would leave out, say, the
aspiration on the "t."

00:27:07.320 --> 00:27:10.050 align:middle line:84%
Because an English voiceless
stop in that position

00:27:10.050 --> 00:27:13.350 align:middle line:84%
is going to be aspirated,
whereas a narrow transcription

00:27:13.350 --> 00:27:16.523 align:middle line:84%
is trying to come as close
as it can to a spectrogram.

00:27:16.523 --> 00:27:17.940 align:middle line:84%
It's going to
represent everything

00:27:17.940 --> 00:27:20.030 align:middle line:90%
about the speech signal.

00:27:20.030 --> 00:27:23.630 align:middle line:84%
I was taught to call that
tight, but apparently I

00:27:23.630 --> 00:27:26.260 align:middle line:90%
was taught by strange people.

00:27:26.260 --> 00:27:27.388 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:27:27.388 --> 00:27:29.180 align:middle line:84%
I knew that I was taught
by strange people.

00:27:29.180 --> 00:27:31.280 align:middle line:84%
I just didn't
realize how strange.

00:27:31.280 --> 00:27:34.440 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions about that?

00:27:34.440 --> 00:27:35.450 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:27:35.450 --> 00:27:36.080 align:middle line:90%
Yawelmani.

00:27:36.080 --> 00:27:37.400 align:middle line:90%
OK, so I'm sorry.

00:27:37.400 --> 00:27:40.110 align:middle line:84%
Let me do the intro
to this slide again.

00:27:40.110 --> 00:27:46.730 align:middle line:84%
So what I just did by taking
you through the English plural

00:27:46.730 --> 00:27:49.143 align:middle line:84%
forms was to try to
introduce you to the idea,

00:27:49.143 --> 00:27:50.810 align:middle line:84%
get you used to the
idea which we're now

00:27:50.810 --> 00:27:53.270 align:middle line:84%
about to see in action,
again, in a different place.

00:27:53.270 --> 00:27:55.550 align:middle line:84%
The idea that when
you are looking

00:27:55.550 --> 00:27:58.130 align:middle line:84%
at a phonological problem,
it is useful to think

00:27:58.130 --> 00:28:01.520 align:middle line:84%
both about what
exactly is happening,

00:28:01.520 --> 00:28:03.060 align:middle line:84%
what sound changes
are happening,

00:28:03.060 --> 00:28:05.930 align:middle line:84%
so in English, here we are
inserting schwas or devoicing

00:28:05.930 --> 00:28:06.630 align:middle line:90%
consonants.

00:28:06.630 --> 00:28:08.480 align:middle line:84%
That's one kind of
thing that's happening.

00:28:08.480 --> 00:28:09.870 align:middle line:90%
It's useful to think about that.

00:28:09.870 --> 00:28:11.880 align:middle line:84%
And it is also useful
to ask yourself,

00:28:11.880 --> 00:28:13.280 align:middle line:90%
why are these things happening?

00:28:13.280 --> 00:28:16.580 align:middle line:84%
Can I think of this as a way
of enforcing a general pattern

00:28:16.580 --> 00:28:18.680 align:middle line:90%
in the language?

00:28:18.680 --> 00:28:22.230 align:middle line:84%
It's useful to think about
both of those things.

00:28:22.230 --> 00:28:25.160 align:middle line:84%
So I'm about to show you another
example of that in action.

00:28:25.160 --> 00:28:28.100 align:middle line:84%
And let me just spoil the
surprise for you right now.

00:28:28.100 --> 00:28:30.770 align:middle line:84%
If there's anybody here who
hates spoilers in movies,

00:28:30.770 --> 00:28:33.740 align:middle line:84%
you should cover your
ears or something.

00:28:33.740 --> 00:28:37.260 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to show you a bunch of
data from a language originally

00:28:37.260 --> 00:28:38.510 align:middle line:90%
spoken in Northern California.

00:28:38.510 --> 00:28:39.843 align:middle line:90%
I believe it's no longer spoken.

00:28:39.843 --> 00:28:41.990 align:middle line:90%
It was called Yawelmani.

00:28:41.990 --> 00:28:46.760 align:middle line:84%
And it was a language that
had a general ban on having

00:28:46.760 --> 00:28:49.310 align:middle line:90%
three consonants in a row.

00:28:49.310 --> 00:28:51.410 align:middle line:84%
Now we're going to
see that general ban

00:28:51.410 --> 00:28:54.200 align:middle line:84%
in a bunch of different
places with different fixes

00:28:54.200 --> 00:28:55.770 align:middle line:90%
in different places.

00:28:55.770 --> 00:29:00.950 align:middle line:84%
So the recognition that these
different things that Yawelmani

00:29:00.950 --> 00:29:02.900 align:middle line:84%
is doing under
different circumstances

00:29:02.900 --> 00:29:07.100 align:middle line:84%
are all instances of this
ban was kind of a discovery,

00:29:07.100 --> 00:29:08.812 align:middle line:84%
and people saying,
yeah, it's doing this,

00:29:08.812 --> 00:29:10.520 align:middle line:84%
and it's doing this,
and it's doing this.

00:29:10.520 --> 00:29:12.380 align:middle line:84%
And what all of
those things achieve

00:29:12.380 --> 00:29:15.350 align:middle line:84%
is avoiding sequences of
three consonants in a row.

00:29:15.350 --> 00:29:18.215 align:middle line:84%
So you guys have an
advantage on the phonologists

00:29:18.215 --> 00:29:22.550 align:middle line:84%
and the Amerindianists who
were studying Yawelmani.

00:29:22.550 --> 00:29:24.770 align:middle line:84%
They were mostly
concentrating on what's

00:29:24.770 --> 00:29:26.300 align:middle line:84%
happening in this form of
the verb, what's happening

00:29:26.300 --> 00:29:27.360 align:middle line:90%
in that form of the verb.

00:29:27.360 --> 00:29:27.860 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:29:27.860 --> 00:29:31.102 align:middle line:84%
But you have the advantage
that you have seen this slide.

00:29:31.102 --> 00:29:32.810 align:middle line:84%
So remember what this
slide says, and now

00:29:32.810 --> 00:29:34.100 align:middle line:90%
let's look at some Yawelmani.

00:29:34.100 --> 00:29:36.620 align:middle line:90%


00:29:36.620 --> 00:29:41.420 align:middle line:84%
Here are a bunch of Yawelmani
verbs in their future forms.

00:29:41.420 --> 00:29:43.610 align:middle line:84%
I don't speak
Yawelmani so I won't

00:29:43.610 --> 00:29:44.910 align:middle line:90%
try to pronounce these for you.

00:29:44.910 --> 00:29:47.040 align:middle line:84%
But you can see that
there's a suffix here,

00:29:47.040 --> 00:29:50.120 align:middle line:84%
which is spelled
E-N. And that suffix

00:29:50.120 --> 00:29:51.800 align:middle line:84%
is the future tense
suffix in Yawelmani.

00:29:51.800 --> 00:29:54.390 align:middle line:90%
So far, so good?

00:29:54.390 --> 00:29:58.380 align:middle line:90%
Now here's the gerund.

00:29:58.380 --> 00:29:59.857 align:middle line:84%
The gerund is a
form of the verb.

00:29:59.857 --> 00:30:02.190 align:middle line:84%
Doesn't matter what the gerund
is, but just so you know,

00:30:02.190 --> 00:30:04.020 align:middle line:84%
a gerund is a form
of the verb that

00:30:04.020 --> 00:30:07.360 align:middle line:84%
allows it to be used as a
noun, turns it into a noun.

00:30:07.360 --> 00:30:10.382 align:middle line:84%
So when you're saying things in
English like "swimming is fun,"

00:30:10.382 --> 00:30:12.090 align:middle line:84%
what you've done is
take the verb "swim,"

00:30:12.090 --> 00:30:14.460 align:middle line:84%
and by adding "-ing," you've
turned it into a gerund,

00:30:14.460 --> 00:30:16.835 align:middle line:84%
that is a noun, the kind of
thing that can be the subject

00:30:16.835 --> 00:30:19.680 align:middle line:84%
of a sentence like
"swimming is fun."

00:30:19.680 --> 00:30:22.000 align:middle line:84%
These are gerunds in
Yawelmani, and as you can see,

00:30:22.000 --> 00:30:23.910 align:middle line:84%
Yawelmani gerunds
involve a suffix, "-taw."

00:30:23.910 --> 00:30:28.350 align:middle line:90%


00:30:28.350 --> 00:30:33.120 align:middle line:84%
Except in that last sequence
of verbs, "sing," "pulverize,"

00:30:33.120 --> 00:30:36.000 align:middle line:84%
and "fight," you
don't just add "-taw."

00:30:36.000 --> 00:30:39.090 align:middle line:84%
You also insert a
vowel, the vowel

00:30:39.090 --> 00:30:40.890 align:middle line:84%
that I'll pronounce
"ee", the vowel that's

00:30:40.890 --> 00:30:45.840 align:middle line:84%
written with the letter "i,"
inside the original verb.

00:30:45.840 --> 00:30:49.230 align:middle line:84%
So you don't get "ilk-taw,"
you get "ilik-taw."

00:30:49.230 --> 00:30:53.780 align:middle line:84%
And you don't get "logw-taw,"
you get "logiw-taw."

00:30:53.780 --> 00:30:55.340 align:middle line:84%
And because you
saw the last slide,

00:30:55.340 --> 00:30:57.612 align:middle line:84%
you know why Yawelmani
is doing this.

00:30:57.612 --> 00:30:59.570 align:middle line:84%
Yawelmani is doing this
because it hates having

00:30:59.570 --> 00:31:01.250 align:middle line:90%
three consonants in a row.

00:31:01.250 --> 00:31:06.167 align:middle line:84%
So it's OK to add "-taw" to
"mut" (swear) or "xat" (eat).

00:31:06.167 --> 00:31:08.000 align:middle line:84%
I said I wouldn't try
to pronounce Yawelmani

00:31:08.000 --> 00:31:09.120 align:middle line:90%
but I guess I lied.

00:31:09.120 --> 00:31:11.120 align:middle line:84%
Because there you're just
putting two consonants

00:31:11.120 --> 00:31:12.660 align:middle line:90%
in a row, and that's fine.

00:31:12.660 --> 00:31:14.330 align:middle line:84%
But "sing,"
"pulverize," "fight,"

00:31:14.330 --> 00:31:16.520 align:middle line:84%
those verbs underlyingly
end in two consonants.

00:31:16.520 --> 00:31:17.510 align:middle line:84%
And if you add
"-taw" after them,

00:31:17.510 --> 00:31:18.800 align:middle line:84%
you're going to have
three consonants in a row,

00:31:18.800 --> 00:31:19.730 align:middle line:90%
and that's bad.

00:31:19.730 --> 00:31:21.772 align:middle line:84%
Yawelmani doesn't like
three consonants in a row,

00:31:21.772 --> 00:31:25.145 align:middle line:90%
so you introduce this vowel.

00:31:25.145 --> 00:31:29.840 align:middle line:84%
Now I just said that's why
Yawelmani is doing this.

00:31:29.840 --> 00:31:32.720 align:middle line:84%
At this stage, you could just
look at this and go, well, no.

00:31:32.720 --> 00:31:33.860 align:middle line:90%
Surely you're overreacting.

00:31:33.860 --> 00:31:36.995 align:middle line:90%
Look, there's a rule.

00:31:36.995 --> 00:31:38.870 align:middle line:84%
If you're forming the
gerund, you add "-taw,"

00:31:38.870 --> 00:31:41.120 align:middle line:84%
and if the verb ends
in two consonants,

00:31:41.120 --> 00:31:44.330 align:middle line:84%
you insert a vowel between
the two consonants as well.

00:31:44.330 --> 00:31:47.010 align:middle line:90%
But wait, there's more.

00:31:47.010 --> 00:31:48.800 align:middle line:90%
So here's one rule.

00:31:48.800 --> 00:31:51.560 align:middle line:84%
Nothingness changes
into an "i," so you

00:31:51.560 --> 00:31:56.150 align:middle line:84%
insert an "i" between the
first and second consonants

00:31:56.150 --> 00:31:58.160 align:middle line:84%
when you have three
consonants in a row.

00:31:58.160 --> 00:32:00.610 align:middle line:90%
That's what that rule says.

00:32:00.610 --> 00:32:02.140 align:middle line:90%
Here's another rule.

00:32:02.140 --> 00:32:03.860 align:middle line:90%
Here's the desiderative.

00:32:03.860 --> 00:32:06.640 align:middle line:84%
So you can take a verb in
Yawelmani and add a suffix,

00:32:06.640 --> 00:32:10.690 align:middle line:84%
"-hatin," that gives you
a new verb that means,

00:32:10.690 --> 00:32:16.540 align:middle line:84%
"want to (verb)," so "want
to know," or "want to sink,"

00:32:16.540 --> 00:32:19.420 align:middle line:84%
refraining from wondering why
you would ever want to sink.

00:32:19.420 --> 00:32:23.190 align:middle line:90%


00:32:23.190 --> 00:32:25.680 align:middle line:84%
The desiderative suffix
is usually "-hatin,"

00:32:25.680 --> 00:32:28.530 align:middle line:84%
except if you're adding it
to a verb that ends in two

00:32:28.530 --> 00:32:30.960 align:middle line:84%
consonants, like
"speak" or "lift," well,

00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:34.620 align:middle line:84%
then the desiderative suffix
is no longer "-hatin."

00:32:34.620 --> 00:32:35.730 align:middle line:90%
It's just "-atin."

00:32:35.730 --> 00:32:39.000 align:middle line:90%
You take off the "h."

00:32:39.000 --> 00:32:39.690 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:32:39.690 --> 00:32:42.240 align:middle line:84%
So here's another thing
that Yawelmani does.

00:32:42.240 --> 00:32:45.810 align:middle line:84%
If you've got two
consonants and then an "h,"

00:32:45.810 --> 00:32:48.510 align:middle line:90%
you get rid of the "h."

00:32:48.510 --> 00:32:50.460 align:middle line:84%
Again, we have two
rules, both fine rules.

00:32:50.460 --> 00:32:52.760 align:middle line:90%
They both work.

00:32:52.760 --> 00:32:55.160 align:middle line:84%
But by just stating them, by
saying, here are two rules,

00:32:55.160 --> 00:32:56.827 align:middle line:84%
here are two things
that Yawelmani does,

00:32:56.827 --> 00:32:59.450 align:middle line:84%
we're obscuring
something we feel like.

00:32:59.450 --> 00:33:01.443 align:middle line:84%
Yawelmani does both
of these things,

00:33:01.443 --> 00:33:03.110 align:middle line:84%
but they both have
the same consequence.

00:33:03.110 --> 00:33:05.112 align:middle line:84%
They are two ways
to avoid strings

00:33:05.112 --> 00:33:06.320 align:middle line:90%
of three consonants in a row.

00:33:06.320 --> 00:33:08.450 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:33:08.450 --> 00:33:10.672 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is this three
consonants [INAUDIBLE]??

00:33:10.672 --> 00:33:13.980 align:middle line:90%


00:33:13.980 --> 00:33:19.050 align:middle line:90%
I see [INAUDIBLE] double Ls.

00:33:19.050 --> 00:33:21.530 align:middle line:90%
I don't really see that.

00:33:21.530 --> 00:33:23.435 align:middle line:84%
Is that actually
just two consonants

00:33:23.435 --> 00:33:24.620 align:middle line:90%
sounds that are [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:24.620 --> 00:33:25.640 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:33:25.640 --> 00:33:27.710 align:middle line:90%
So it's two, yes.

00:33:27.710 --> 00:33:30.050 align:middle line:90%
It is a geminate "l."

00:33:30.050 --> 00:33:33.920 align:middle line:84%
I say this out of my vast,
vast knowledge of Yawelmani.

00:33:33.920 --> 00:33:35.457 align:middle line:90%
I'm being ironic.

00:33:35.457 --> 00:33:37.040 align:middle line:84%
I don't know anything
about Yawelmani,

00:33:37.040 --> 00:33:38.930 align:middle line:90%
but this is a geminate "l."

00:33:38.930 --> 00:33:41.270 align:middle line:84%
It's an "l" sound that's
held for two beats.

00:33:41.270 --> 00:33:44.120 align:middle line:84%
So yes, we need that to
count as two consonants.

00:33:44.120 --> 00:33:45.690 align:middle line:84%
You're raising a
really good point.

00:33:45.690 --> 00:33:46.820 align:middle line:84%
We have to ask
ourselves, when we

00:33:46.820 --> 00:33:48.987 align:middle line:84%
say it's bad to have three
consonants in a row, what

00:33:48.987 --> 00:33:50.550 align:middle line:90%
counts as a consonant?

00:33:50.550 --> 00:33:52.460 align:middle line:90%
This had better be one.

00:33:52.460 --> 00:33:54.400 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:33:54.400 --> 00:33:56.460 align:middle line:90%
Good question.

00:33:56.460 --> 00:33:58.170 align:middle line:90%
All right, more Yawelmani.

00:33:58.170 --> 00:34:02.670 align:middle line:90%


00:34:02.670 --> 00:34:06.840 align:middle line:84%
Here is a passive
suffix, spelled H-N-E-L.

00:34:06.840 --> 00:34:08.500 align:middle line:90%
I won't attempt to pronounce it.

00:34:08.500 --> 00:34:10.830 align:middle line:84%
So here we've got
the verb "to be tied"

00:34:10.830 --> 00:34:12.330 align:middle line:90%
and the verb "to be hit."

00:34:12.330 --> 00:34:13.980 align:middle line:84%
So you can take
the verb "to tie,"

00:34:13.980 --> 00:34:17.050 align:middle line:84%
"t'ik'e," which has two
of those cool ejectives,

00:34:17.050 --> 00:34:23.050 align:middle line:84%
and you can add this passive
suffix to it, H-N-E-L.

00:34:23.050 --> 00:34:26.659 align:middle line:84%
Maybe all of you can imagine
what's going to happen next.

00:34:26.659 --> 00:34:29.710 align:middle line:84%
If we add this suffix to
something that already ends

00:34:29.710 --> 00:34:32.980 align:middle line:84%
in a consonant, well,
then we get rid of the H.

00:34:32.980 --> 00:34:36.438 align:middle line:84%
So "to be helped" or "to
be held under the arm,"

00:34:36.438 --> 00:34:38.230 align:middle line:84%
those are two verbs
that end in consonants,

00:34:38.230 --> 00:34:41.889 align:middle line:84%
and now the suffix
is just "-nel."

00:34:41.889 --> 00:34:44.440 align:middle line:84%
So in the last slide, we
saw that "h" goes away

00:34:44.440 --> 00:34:46.389 align:middle line:84%
if it's preceded
by two consonants.

00:34:46.389 --> 00:34:48.909 align:middle line:84%
In this slide, we see
that "h" goes away

00:34:48.909 --> 00:34:51.760 align:middle line:84%
if it has consonants
on either side of it.

00:34:51.760 --> 00:34:53.810 align:middle line:90%
Fine, we can say it that way.

00:34:53.810 --> 00:34:57.162 align:middle line:84%
We can state different rules
for all of these morphemes.

00:34:57.162 --> 00:34:59.620 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, the first people
who worked on Yawelmani money

00:34:59.620 --> 00:35:00.237 align:middle line:90%
did just that.

00:35:00.237 --> 00:35:02.320 align:middle line:84%
They were like, OK, the
passive suffix is "-hnel,"

00:35:02.320 --> 00:35:04.780 align:middle line:84%
unless the verb
ends in a consonant,

00:35:04.780 --> 00:35:05.860 align:middle line:90%
and then it's "-nel."

00:35:05.860 --> 00:35:07.960 align:middle line:84%
The desiderative
suffix is "-hatin,"

00:35:07.960 --> 00:35:10.490 align:middle line:84%
unless the verb ends
in two consonants,

00:35:10.490 --> 00:35:12.430 align:middle line:90%
and then it's "-atin."

00:35:12.430 --> 00:35:17.360 align:middle line:84%
You can do that but you're
missing a generalization, which

00:35:17.360 --> 00:35:19.460 align:middle line:84%
is Yawelmani is a
language that hates having

00:35:19.460 --> 00:35:22.860 align:middle line:90%
three consonants in a row.

00:35:22.860 --> 00:35:25.017 align:middle line:84%
So this is like the
distinction that we

00:35:25.017 --> 00:35:27.350 align:middle line:84%
were drawing when we were
talking about English plurals.

00:35:27.350 --> 00:35:29.142 align:middle line:84%
When we were talking
about English plurals,

00:35:29.142 --> 00:35:31.490 align:middle line:84%
we said, yeah,
English doesn't like

00:35:31.490 --> 00:35:34.790 align:middle line:84%
words that end in two
stridents, or words

00:35:34.790 --> 00:35:37.550 align:middle line:84%
that end in a voiced
sound preceded

00:35:37.550 --> 00:35:40.340 align:middle line:84%
by a voiceless sounding, which
doesn't allow words like that.

00:35:40.340 --> 00:35:43.910 align:middle line:84%
And English has repairs,
things it does, it devoices,

00:35:43.910 --> 00:35:45.587 align:middle line:90%
or it introduces a schwa.

00:35:45.587 --> 00:35:47.420 align:middle line:84%
We're seeing something
similar in Yawelmani.

00:35:47.420 --> 00:35:47.920 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:35:47.920 --> 00:35:51.760 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So in two examples,
we had "-hatin" and "-nel,"

00:35:51.760 --> 00:35:53.283 align:middle line:90%
which were both [INAUDIBLE].

00:35:53.283 --> 00:35:54.200 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:35:54.200 --> 00:36:03.670 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE],, should
we try to go through the sounds

00:36:03.670 --> 00:36:04.210 align:middle line:90%
itself?

00:36:04.210 --> 00:36:07.600 align:middle line:84%
Or would this make
someone who's trying

00:36:07.600 --> 00:36:11.050 align:middle line:84%
to come up with the rules mad
to consider something like,

00:36:11.050 --> 00:36:13.780 align:middle line:84%
if "h" is part of
the suffix, then

00:36:13.780 --> 00:36:16.300 align:middle line:84%
drop the "h," instead
of the position of "h,"

00:36:16.300 --> 00:36:18.420 align:middle line:90%
the three consonants?

00:36:18.420 --> 00:36:20.920 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I'm sorry, can
you ask your question again?

00:36:20.920 --> 00:36:22.087 align:middle line:90%
I'm not sure I'm getting it.

00:36:22.087 --> 00:36:23.120 align:middle line:90%
Can you just say again?

00:36:23.120 --> 00:36:25.840 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So in "-hatin"
and "-nel" example,

00:36:25.840 --> 00:36:30.810 align:middle line:84%
the conclusion was about
[INAUDIBLE] and the three

00:36:30.810 --> 00:36:31.605 align:middle line:90%
consonants.

00:36:31.605 --> 00:36:34.280 align:middle line:90%


00:36:34.280 --> 00:36:37.130 align:middle line:84%
Could it also be
about whether "h"

00:36:37.130 --> 00:36:39.095 align:middle line:90%
is part of the added suffix?

00:36:39.095 --> 00:36:40.220 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, I see.

00:36:40.220 --> 00:36:43.437 align:middle line:90%
I see, I see.

00:36:43.437 --> 00:36:44.270 align:middle line:90%
That's a nice point.

00:36:44.270 --> 00:36:51.070 align:middle line:84%
So could you say, suffixes
that begin with an "h"

00:36:51.070 --> 00:36:58.360 align:middle line:84%
won't begin with an "h" if
the "h" is going to create

00:36:58.360 --> 00:37:00.040 align:middle line:90%
a sequence of three consonants?

00:37:00.040 --> 00:37:02.410 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:37:02.410 --> 00:37:07.600 align:middle line:84%
Yes, indeed, that
covers everything

00:37:07.600 --> 00:37:11.110 align:middle line:84%
I did with my last two
rules, though it's not

00:37:11.110 --> 00:37:15.070 align:middle line:84%
something that's easy to
state with the rule formalism

00:37:15.070 --> 00:37:17.500 align:middle line:90%
that I showed you.

00:37:17.500 --> 00:37:20.110 align:middle line:84%
Maybe the response to that
is, so we need a better rule

00:37:20.110 --> 00:37:22.090 align:middle line:90%
formalism.

00:37:22.090 --> 00:37:26.440 align:middle line:90%
But no, that's absolutely right.

00:37:26.440 --> 00:37:28.180 align:middle line:84%
Really what you're
asking, in a way,

00:37:28.180 --> 00:37:31.180 align:middle line:84%
is, is it important
that those examples

00:37:31.180 --> 00:37:33.820 align:middle line:84%
involved in "h" that was at
the beginning of a suffix?

00:37:33.820 --> 00:37:35.410 align:middle line:84%
Could I give you
any examples where

00:37:35.410 --> 00:37:40.030 align:middle line:84%
there was a verb that ended
in two consonants, let's say,

00:37:40.030 --> 00:37:42.850 align:middle line:90%
of which the last was an "h"?

00:37:42.850 --> 00:37:45.850 align:middle line:84%
And then when you add a
suffix, the "h' will go away.

00:37:45.850 --> 00:37:48.250 align:middle line:84%
That's what these
rules are predicting.

00:37:48.250 --> 00:37:50.630 align:middle line:84%
And your rule is
predicting something else.

00:37:50.630 --> 00:37:52.530 align:middle line:84%
And I don't know
enough about Yawelmani

00:37:52.530 --> 00:37:54.493 align:middle line:84%
to know whether there
are examples that allow

00:37:54.493 --> 00:37:55.660 align:middle line:90%
you to draw the distinction.

00:37:55.660 --> 00:37:58.035 align:middle line:84%
But you're absolutely right,
that's what you'd want to go

00:37:58.035 --> 00:38:00.280 align:middle line:84%
do is to find out whether
the suffix matters.

00:38:00.280 --> 00:38:03.380 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So if
you have a suffix

00:38:03.380 --> 00:38:06.263 align:middle line:84%
which has "h" in the second
place of a consonant--

00:38:06.263 --> 00:38:08.680 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: That would
be another good place to look,

00:38:08.680 --> 00:38:09.180 align:middle line:90%
wouldn't it?

00:38:09.180 --> 00:38:13.010 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: --in that case, would
the "h" drop, or [INAUDIBLE]??

00:38:13.010 --> 00:38:16.520 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So yeah, let's
make up a Yawelmani consonant.

00:38:16.520 --> 00:38:20.870 align:middle line:84%
If there were a
consonant in H-E-L,

00:38:20.870 --> 00:38:25.190 align:middle line:84%
I guess these rules predict
that if you added that

00:38:25.190 --> 00:38:27.500 align:middle line:84%
to a verb that
ended in a consonant

00:38:27.500 --> 00:38:30.740 align:middle line:90%
that the "h" would drop.

00:38:30.740 --> 00:38:33.350 align:middle line:84%
And so that's what
these rules predict.

00:38:33.350 --> 00:38:35.450 align:middle line:84%
The imaginable rule
that you're talking

00:38:35.450 --> 00:38:38.120 align:middle line:84%
about that says "h" at
the beginning of a suffix

00:38:38.120 --> 00:38:40.713 align:middle line:84%
is particularly
vulnerable, and you'll

00:38:40.713 --> 00:38:42.380 align:middle line:84%
drop an "h" at the
beginning of a suffix

00:38:42.380 --> 00:38:44.330 align:middle line:84%
as a way of fixing
this general rule.

00:38:44.330 --> 00:38:47.430 align:middle line:84%
And then if you can't do
that, then you insert a vowel.

00:38:47.430 --> 00:38:48.930 align:middle line:84%
Maybe that's a way
to talk about it.

00:38:48.930 --> 00:38:50.305 align:middle line:84%
That makes a
different prediction

00:38:50.305 --> 00:38:51.665 align:middle line:90%
about this kind of suffix.

00:38:51.665 --> 00:38:54.290 align:middle line:84%
I'm pretty sure there aren't any
suffixes that have that shape.

00:38:54.290 --> 00:38:55.550 align:middle line:84%
I don't know much
about Yawelmani

00:38:55.550 --> 00:38:57.800 align:middle line:84%
but I'm pretty sure there
aren't any things like that.

00:38:57.800 --> 00:39:01.730 align:middle line:84%
But all of us should now
go find things on Yawelmani

00:39:01.730 --> 00:39:02.360 align:middle line:90%
and read them.

00:39:02.360 --> 00:39:04.297 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so this stuff
is all out there.

00:39:04.297 --> 00:39:05.630 align:middle line:90%
It'd be interesting to find out.

00:39:05.630 --> 00:39:07.088 align:middle line:84%
But you're right,
this is exactly--

00:39:07.088 --> 00:39:09.380 align:middle line:84%
what you're doing is exactly
what we want to do now.

00:39:09.380 --> 00:39:11.450 align:middle line:84%
So I'm showing you
one set of rules

00:39:11.450 --> 00:39:12.710 align:middle line:90%
that would cover these data.

00:39:12.710 --> 00:39:14.690 align:middle line:84%
And it's worth asking yourself,
what other kinds of rules

00:39:14.690 --> 00:39:15.648 align:middle line:90%
would cover these data?

00:39:15.648 --> 00:39:18.020 align:middle line:84%
And how would we find
out which of these rules

00:39:18.020 --> 00:39:19.040 align:middle line:90%
are the right ones?

00:39:19.040 --> 00:39:21.260 align:middle line:84%
And the only answer
is the boring one,

00:39:21.260 --> 00:39:23.090 align:middle line:84%
which is, go learn
more about Yawelmani.

00:39:23.090 --> 00:39:25.790 align:middle line:84%
But you're right, that's
the first thing to do.

00:39:25.790 --> 00:39:27.830 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, my
main question now

00:39:27.830 --> 00:39:30.410 align:middle line:84%
is more of the lines
of what you said.

00:39:30.410 --> 00:39:32.940 align:middle line:84%
This rule of formalism doesn't
allow these kinds of rules,

00:39:32.940 --> 00:39:35.090 align:middle line:84%
right, so do we just
not want to [INAUDIBLE]??

00:39:35.090 --> 00:39:35.510 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: No.

00:39:35.510 --> 00:39:36.560 align:middle line:90%
Well, so we want to find out.

00:39:36.560 --> 00:39:37.925 align:middle line:84%
Because if there
are rules-- you're

00:39:37.925 --> 00:39:39.150 align:middle line:90%
raising a really good point.

00:39:39.150 --> 00:39:41.210 align:middle line:84%
If there are rules
like that, then we

00:39:41.210 --> 00:39:45.080 align:middle line:84%
want a rule formalism that
allows us to state them.

00:39:45.080 --> 00:39:47.690 align:middle line:84%
It's not like we would have to
kill ourselves making that rule

00:39:47.690 --> 00:39:48.500 align:middle line:90%
formalism either.

00:39:48.500 --> 00:39:50.150 align:middle line:84%
So the rule formalism
that I showed

00:39:50.150 --> 00:39:54.350 align:middle line:84%
you has a symbol in it
for a word boundary that

00:39:54.350 --> 00:39:56.630 align:middle line:84%
says there are
special things that

00:39:56.630 --> 00:39:59.450 align:middle line:90%
happen at the end of a word.

00:39:59.450 --> 00:40:03.873 align:middle line:84%
So there's this symbol
that says, in English,

00:40:03.873 --> 00:40:06.290 align:middle line:84%
if you have two stridents at
the end of a word then you've

00:40:06.290 --> 00:40:09.410 align:middle line:84%
done something wrong and
you must introduce a schwa.

00:40:09.410 --> 00:40:13.850 align:middle line:84%
No reason we couldn't introduce
a symbol for morpheme boundary.

00:40:13.850 --> 00:40:17.870 align:middle line:84%
And then we would use it to
capture your generalization.

00:40:17.870 --> 00:40:20.618 align:middle line:84%
In order to find out whether
we need that for Yawelmani,

00:40:20.618 --> 00:40:22.160 align:middle line:84%
well, we need to
know more Yawelmani.

00:40:22.160 --> 00:40:24.743 align:middle line:84%
But the question you're raising
is exactly the right question,

00:40:24.743 --> 00:40:28.250 align:middle line:84%
and it's how we find out whether
we need devices like that.

00:40:28.250 --> 00:40:29.210 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, cool.

00:40:29.210 --> 00:40:30.870 align:middle line:84%
That's linguistics
you're doing there.

00:40:30.870 --> 00:40:31.370 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:40:31.370 --> 00:40:33.162 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What exactly
are the apostrophes?

00:40:33.162 --> 00:40:35.090 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Oh, good question.

00:40:35.090 --> 00:40:39.720 align:middle line:84%
These particular apostrophes
are markers of ejective stops.

00:40:39.720 --> 00:40:47.240 align:middle line:84%
So the verb to hit is
"tok-o" with an ejective "k."

00:40:47.240 --> 00:40:50.450 align:middle line:90%
That's what those are.

00:40:50.450 --> 00:40:52.040 align:middle line:90%
People remember ejective stops.

00:40:52.040 --> 00:40:54.350 align:middle line:84%
All of you were
ejectively stopping at me

00:40:54.350 --> 00:40:56.780 align:middle line:84%
during the phonetics
part of the class.

00:40:56.780 --> 00:40:58.970 align:middle line:84%
This is where you
make a glottal stop

00:40:58.970 --> 00:41:02.540 align:middle line:84%
and express some air from
your mouth during a stop

00:41:02.540 --> 00:41:07.220 align:middle line:84%
by moving your larynx, shoving
the air out that way, "ka."

00:41:07.220 --> 00:41:09.140 align:middle line:90%
OK, cool.

00:41:09.140 --> 00:41:13.370 align:middle line:84%
So to summarize then,
we have these rules

00:41:13.370 --> 00:41:17.930 align:middle line:84%
and this interesting discussion
about whether they're

00:41:17.930 --> 00:41:18.650 align:middle line:90%
the right rules.

00:41:18.650 --> 00:41:22.370 align:middle line:84%
There are various ways of
categorizing the rules.

00:41:22.370 --> 00:41:27.260 align:middle line:84%
And what I'm suggesting
here is that we

00:41:27.260 --> 00:41:29.210 align:middle line:84%
might want to be
willing to abstract away

00:41:29.210 --> 00:41:31.580 align:middle line:84%
from the particular rules
that I posited and say,

00:41:31.580 --> 00:41:34.910 align:middle line:84%
Yawelmani doesn't like strings
of three consonants in a row.

00:41:34.910 --> 00:41:37.490 align:middle line:84%
And it has these general
principles that say,

00:41:37.490 --> 00:41:40.610 align:middle line:84%
you can introduce a vowel
to break up sequences,

00:41:40.610 --> 00:41:41.917 align:middle line:90%
and you can get rid of an "h."

00:41:41.917 --> 00:41:44.000 align:middle line:84%
And the question that was
getting raised over here

00:41:44.000 --> 00:41:46.880 align:middle line:84%
was, is it just, you can
always get rid of an "h"?

00:41:46.880 --> 00:41:48.470 align:middle line:84%
Or is it, you can
get rid of an "h,"

00:41:48.470 --> 00:41:50.960 align:middle line:84%
but only if it's the
beginning of a suffix?

00:41:50.960 --> 00:41:53.510 align:middle line:84%
Because that was the case
in all of the examples

00:41:53.510 --> 00:41:56.180 align:middle line:84%
that I showed you, to
which, unfortunately,

00:41:56.180 --> 00:41:58.430 align:middle line:84%
the only answer is, go
ask someone who knows more

00:41:58.430 --> 00:42:00.440 align:middle line:84%
Yawelmani than me,
which is most people.

00:42:00.440 --> 00:42:01.470 align:middle line:90%
No, that's not true.

00:42:01.470 --> 00:42:05.330 align:middle line:84%
There are several people who
know more Yawelmani than me.

00:42:05.330 --> 00:42:06.800 align:middle line:90%
So do people see this?

00:42:06.800 --> 00:42:08.480 align:middle line:84%
We're making the same
move here that we

00:42:08.480 --> 00:42:10.040 align:middle line:84%
made when we were talking
about the English plurals.

00:42:10.040 --> 00:42:12.740 align:middle line:84%
So we said, yeah, English
has these particular things

00:42:12.740 --> 00:42:14.270 align:middle line:90%
that it does, these repairs.

00:42:14.270 --> 00:42:17.210 align:middle line:84%
And these repairs are there
because of these conditions

00:42:17.210 --> 00:42:19.250 align:middle line:84%
on English phonotactics,
rules about how

00:42:19.250 --> 00:42:20.585 align:middle line:90%
sounds can combine in English.

00:42:20.585 --> 00:42:21.710 align:middle line:90%
Similar kind of thing here.

00:42:21.710 --> 00:42:24.260 align:middle line:84%
There's a principle of
Yawelmani phonotactics.

00:42:24.260 --> 00:42:25.880 align:middle line:84%
Don't have three
consonants in a row,

00:42:25.880 --> 00:42:27.380 align:middle line:84%
and there are these
repairs that you

00:42:27.380 --> 00:42:31.100 align:middle line:84%
use to repair violations of
them that you have created

00:42:31.100 --> 00:42:34.340 align:middle line:90%
by adding suffixes to things.

00:42:34.340 --> 00:42:37.930 align:middle line:90%


00:42:37.930 --> 00:42:42.910 align:middle line:84%
Yawelmani was the first
example that people first--

00:42:42.910 --> 00:42:46.990 align:middle line:84%
made people think about
phonological problems this way.

00:42:46.990 --> 00:42:50.290 align:middle line:84%
It was described
as a conspiracy,

00:42:50.290 --> 00:42:53.920 align:middle line:84%
that is, the
phonologists who first

00:42:53.920 --> 00:42:55.967 align:middle line:84%
noticed this about
Yawelmani were saying,

00:42:55.967 --> 00:42:58.300 align:middle line:84%
yes, we have all these rules
and they do various things.

00:42:58.300 --> 00:43:00.400 align:middle line:84%
But they all have the
consequence that you get rid

00:43:00.400 --> 00:43:02.140 align:middle line:90%
of three consonant sequences.

00:43:02.140 --> 00:43:04.060 align:middle line:84%
And so it's as though
there's a conspiracy,

00:43:04.060 --> 00:43:06.130 align:middle line:84%
and all of these different
rules are conspiring

00:43:06.130 --> 00:43:08.200 align:middle line:84%
to have the consequence
that you never

00:43:08.200 --> 00:43:09.520 align:middle line:90%
have three consonants in a row.

00:43:09.520 --> 00:43:10.975 align:middle line:84%
And then we might
want to know, why?

00:43:10.975 --> 00:43:13.183 align:middle line:84%
Why doesn't it like having
three consonants in a row?

00:43:13.183 --> 00:43:14.900 align:middle line:90%
There's all kinds of work to do.

00:43:14.900 --> 00:43:18.400 align:middle line:84%
I want to show you another
example of a conspiracy.

00:43:18.400 --> 00:43:20.350 align:middle line:84%
And we can probably
get some ways into this

00:43:20.350 --> 00:43:22.060 align:middle line:90%
before we have to stop.

00:43:22.060 --> 00:43:23.800 align:middle line:90%
This one is from Japanese.

00:43:23.800 --> 00:43:25.360 align:middle line:84%
So in order to show
you this, I need

00:43:25.360 --> 00:43:26.410 align:middle line:84%
to tell you some
things about how

00:43:26.410 --> 00:43:27.580 align:middle line:90%
Japanese pronunciation goes.

00:43:27.580 --> 00:43:32.350 align:middle line:84%
This is all going to be from
the Tokyo dialect of Japanese.

00:43:32.350 --> 00:43:36.630 align:middle line:84%
So this is how pitch
accent works in Japanese.

00:43:36.630 --> 00:43:40.260 align:middle line:90%
So here are four words.

00:43:40.260 --> 00:43:42.120 align:middle line:84%
Does anybody here speak
Japanese natively?

00:43:42.120 --> 00:43:43.350 align:middle line:90%
Any Japanese people here?

00:43:43.350 --> 00:43:44.970 align:middle line:90%
Oh, cool.

00:43:44.970 --> 00:43:46.980 align:middle line:84%
Can you pronounce
these words for us?

00:43:46.980 --> 00:43:51.253 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: "Makura-wa,
kokoro-wa, atama-wa, sakana-wa."

00:43:51.253 --> 00:43:52.170 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Cool.

00:43:52.170 --> 00:43:56.520 align:middle line:84%
So I just broke my
heart listening to you

00:43:56.520 --> 00:43:57.780 align:middle line:90%
say those words that way.

00:43:57.780 --> 00:43:59.370 align:middle line:84%
There are various ways
to pronounce these words.

00:43:59.370 --> 00:44:00.912 align:middle line:84%
Let me pronounce
them a different way

00:44:00.912 --> 00:44:03.810 align:middle line:84%
and then you can tell me
why I pronounced them wrong.

00:44:03.810 --> 00:44:07.560 align:middle line:84%
The first one I think we
agreed on, it's "makura-wa."

00:44:07.560 --> 00:44:09.960 align:middle line:84%
There are speakers who will
pronounce the second one

00:44:09.960 --> 00:44:14.850 align:middle line:84%
"kokoro-wa," and the third one,
"atama-wa," and the last one,

00:44:14.850 --> 00:44:16.640 align:middle line:90%
"sakana-wa."

00:44:16.640 --> 00:44:20.090 align:middle line:84%
But for you, that's apparently--
so you were pronouncing

00:44:20.090 --> 00:44:22.100 align:middle line:84%
all but the first
one the same way,

00:44:22.100 --> 00:44:25.400 align:middle line:84%
I think, low on the first one,
and high on all of the others.

00:44:25.400 --> 00:44:29.180 align:middle line:84%
This is a point of variation
between dialects of Japanese.

00:44:29.180 --> 00:44:31.250 align:middle line:84%
So people from different
parts of Japan,

00:44:31.250 --> 00:44:33.473 align:middle line:84%
when I was living in
Japan, I would talk

00:44:33.473 --> 00:44:34.640 align:middle line:90%
about going to other places.

00:44:34.640 --> 00:44:37.880 align:middle line:84%
And the people I was living
in Tokyo, the people in Tokyo

00:44:37.880 --> 00:44:40.090 align:middle line:84%
would say, oh,
they speak Japanese

00:44:40.090 --> 00:44:41.090 align:middle line:90%
so strangely over there.

00:44:41.090 --> 00:44:44.937 align:middle line:84%
They don't say "HAshi,"
they say "haSHI."

00:44:44.937 --> 00:44:46.770 align:middle line:84%
They put the high tone
in a different place,

00:44:46.770 --> 00:44:48.270 align:middle line:84%
put high pitch in
a different place.

00:44:48.270 --> 00:44:52.790 align:middle line:90%


00:44:52.790 --> 00:44:54.380 align:middle line:84%
So I want to show
you some things

00:44:54.380 --> 00:44:56.540 align:middle line:84%
about how accent
works in Japanese,

00:44:56.540 --> 00:44:58.138 align:middle line:90%
how accent is pronounced.

00:44:58.138 --> 00:44:59.930 align:middle line:84%
So first, I'm going to
tell you some things

00:44:59.930 --> 00:45:02.030 align:middle line:84%
about the laws of
accent realization,

00:45:02.030 --> 00:45:03.410 align:middle line:90%
the way I was taught them.

00:45:03.410 --> 00:45:06.623 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm curious, actually,
where are you from, where?

00:45:06.623 --> 00:45:07.790 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I'm half-Japanese.

00:45:07.790 --> 00:45:12.980 align:middle line:84%
My mom's from Japan, so I
just went to a Japanese school

00:45:12.980 --> 00:45:13.550 align:middle line:90%
in the US.

00:45:13.550 --> 00:45:14.030 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, wow.

00:45:14.030 --> 00:45:14.420 align:middle line:90%
Awesome.

00:45:14.420 --> 00:45:15.020 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

00:45:15.020 --> 00:45:17.240 align:middle line:84%
That's cool that you
speak Japanese so well.

00:45:17.240 --> 00:45:20.930 align:middle line:84%
My son, whose
mother is Japanese,

00:45:20.930 --> 00:45:22.890 align:middle line:84%
is attempting to follow
in your footsteps.

00:45:22.890 --> 00:45:23.390 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:45:23.390 --> 00:45:25.920 align:middle line:84%
I have to get you to tell
me how it worked for you.

00:45:25.920 --> 00:45:26.420 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

00:45:26.420 --> 00:45:27.878 align:middle line:84%
So let me tell you
something things

00:45:27.878 --> 00:45:31.700 align:middle line:84%
about how accent
realization works.

00:45:31.700 --> 00:45:34.310 align:middle line:84%
By accent, we're talking
about the pitch accent

00:45:34.310 --> 00:45:35.300 align:middle line:90%
system of Japanese.

00:45:35.300 --> 00:45:37.142 align:middle line:84%
So if you decide
to study Japanese,

00:45:37.142 --> 00:45:38.600 align:middle line:84%
one of the things
you must learn is

00:45:38.600 --> 00:45:41.330 align:middle line:84%
where the pitch of your
voice needs to be high

00:45:41.330 --> 00:45:44.660 align:middle line:90%
and where it needs to be low.

00:45:44.660 --> 00:45:48.960 align:middle line:84%
And the basic rules for this
in Tokyo Japanese go like this.

00:45:48.960 --> 00:45:53.930 align:middle line:84%
There are words that don't
have an accent anywhere.

00:45:53.930 --> 00:45:57.680 align:middle line:84%
The example on the slide here
is "fish," the last word.

00:45:57.680 --> 00:46:01.610 align:middle line:84%
And in words like that,
the first syllable is low

00:46:01.610 --> 00:46:02.990 align:middle line:90%
and the others are high.

00:46:02.990 --> 00:46:06.652 align:middle line:84%
So you get "saKANA-WA,"
low, high, high, high.

00:46:06.652 --> 00:46:09.550 align:middle line:90%


00:46:09.550 --> 00:46:12.490 align:middle line:84%
And then, when there
is accent, the accent

00:46:12.490 --> 00:46:16.510 align:middle line:84%
perturbs this distribution
of lows and highs

00:46:16.510 --> 00:46:20.810 align:middle line:84%
in a particular way that I
now tried to describe for you.

00:46:20.810 --> 00:46:22.870 align:middle line:90%
It goes like this.

00:46:22.870 --> 00:46:26.080 align:middle line:84%
The accented syllable
is high, so "pillow"

00:46:26.080 --> 00:46:27.670 align:middle line:84%
has accent on the
first syllable.

00:46:27.670 --> 00:46:29.620 align:middle line:84%
"Heart" has accent on
the second syllable.

00:46:29.620 --> 00:46:32.260 align:middle line:84%
"Head" has accent on
the third syllable.

00:46:32.260 --> 00:46:35.170 align:middle line:84%
So you get a high on
the accented syllable,

00:46:35.170 --> 00:46:38.500 align:middle line:84%
and every syllable after an
accented syllable is low.

00:46:38.500 --> 00:46:42.100 align:middle line:84%
So pillow is "MAkura-wa,"
high, low, low, low,

00:46:42.100 --> 00:46:45.160 align:middle line:84%
because it's got accent
on the first syllable.

00:46:45.160 --> 00:46:48.790 align:middle line:84%
And then, heart and
head have accent

00:46:48.790 --> 00:46:51.245 align:middle line:84%
on places that are not
the first syllable.

00:46:51.245 --> 00:46:52.870 align:middle line:84%
And so they have high
on their accented

00:46:52.870 --> 00:46:54.650 align:middle line:84%
syllable and then
low from then on.

00:46:54.650 --> 00:46:59.530 align:middle line:84%
So heart is "koKOro-wa,"
low, high, low, low.

00:46:59.530 --> 00:47:01.270 align:middle line:84%
Low on the first
syllable because, well,

00:47:01.270 --> 00:47:03.370 align:middle line:84%
low on the first syllable
is the general thing,

00:47:03.370 --> 00:47:08.380 align:middle line:84%
and high on the second syllable,
and then low from then on.

00:47:08.380 --> 00:47:08.980 align:middle line:90%
Don't worry.

00:47:08.980 --> 00:47:10.420 align:middle line:84%
You're not going to
be quizzed on this.

00:47:10.420 --> 00:47:12.170 align:middle line:84%
Actually, I'm really
just telling you this

00:47:12.170 --> 00:47:14.350 align:middle line:84%
so that you will know why
we might care about where

00:47:14.350 --> 00:47:15.540 align:middle line:90%
accent goes in Japanese.

00:47:15.540 --> 00:47:18.040 align:middle line:84%
Mostly what we're going to be
talking about in just a second

00:47:18.040 --> 00:47:20.875 align:middle line:84%
is what the rules are for
where accent goes in Japanese.

00:47:20.875 --> 00:47:23.500 align:middle line:84%
Though if none of this makes any
sense, or if you're wondering,

00:47:23.500 --> 00:47:25.990 align:middle line:84%
why on Earth are we talking
about all these highs and lows?

00:47:25.990 --> 00:47:28.365 align:middle line:84%
It's really just so you'll
know what we're talking about.

00:47:28.365 --> 00:47:30.820 align:middle line:84%
We're talking about what the
rules are for what goes high

00:47:30.820 --> 00:47:33.190 align:middle line:90%
and what goes low in Japanese.

00:47:33.190 --> 00:47:37.030 align:middle line:84%
So there are these regular
rules that go for "fish."

00:47:37.030 --> 00:47:38.980 align:middle line:84%
It's like, if you have
an unaccented word

00:47:38.980 --> 00:47:41.770 align:middle line:84%
it's low at the beginning
and high from then on.

00:47:41.770 --> 00:47:44.410 align:middle line:84%
If you have any
accents, the basic rule

00:47:44.410 --> 00:47:47.350 align:middle line:84%
is, put a high on the accented
syllable, low from then

00:47:47.350 --> 00:47:50.140 align:middle line:84%
on, and otherwise,
act like a fish.

00:47:50.140 --> 00:47:53.133 align:middle line:84%
So "pillow" has a high
tone at the beginning

00:47:53.133 --> 00:47:55.300 align:middle line:84%
because it's accented on
the first syllable and then

00:47:55.300 --> 00:47:57.010 align:middle line:84%
it's low from then
on, "MAkura-wa."

00:47:57.010 --> 00:48:00.010 align:middle line:84%
And then, everything else tries
to be low, high, high, high,

00:48:00.010 --> 00:48:01.612 align:middle line:84%
with the overriding
principle that you

00:48:01.612 --> 00:48:03.820 align:middle line:84%
have to have a high on your
accented syllable and low

00:48:03.820 --> 00:48:04.540 align:middle line:90%
from then on.

00:48:04.540 --> 00:48:07.420 align:middle line:84%
That's the basic
way that this works.

00:48:07.420 --> 00:48:09.580 align:middle line:84%
And I promised Raquel that
when we got to Japanese

00:48:09.580 --> 00:48:13.690 align:middle line:84%
I would tell you about a way
in which languages sometimes

00:48:13.690 --> 00:48:17.320 align:middle line:84%
distinguish verbs from nouns
and other kinds of things.

00:48:17.320 --> 00:48:24.500 align:middle line:84%
Japanese nouns, as you can
see, get to have accent or not.

00:48:24.500 --> 00:48:28.090 align:middle line:84%
So "fish" doesn't have an accent
and the rest of these words

00:48:28.090 --> 00:48:28.930 align:middle line:90%
have accents.

00:48:28.930 --> 00:48:31.120 align:middle line:84%
And if they have an
accent, the accent

00:48:31.120 --> 00:48:34.420 align:middle line:84%
can be on any
syllable of the word.

00:48:34.420 --> 00:48:38.110 align:middle line:84%
Verbs in Japanese only get to
choose whether they're accented

00:48:38.110 --> 00:48:38.710 align:middle line:90%
or not.

00:48:38.710 --> 00:48:41.290 align:middle line:84%
They don't get to choose
where the accent is.

00:48:41.290 --> 00:48:43.163 align:middle line:84%
And that's not
uncommon for languages

00:48:43.163 --> 00:48:44.830 align:middle line:84%
to make that kind of
distinction to have

00:48:44.830 --> 00:48:48.520 align:middle line:84%
a richer array of possible
accent types in nouns

00:48:48.520 --> 00:48:50.510 align:middle line:90%
than in verbs.

00:48:50.510 --> 00:48:53.860 align:middle line:84%
That's one of the kinds
of noun-verb phonological

00:48:53.860 --> 00:48:56.380 align:middle line:84%
differences people
sometimes settle on.

00:48:56.380 --> 00:48:58.713 align:middle line:84%
So really, this is just here
for your greater education.

00:48:58.713 --> 00:49:00.880 align:middle line:84%
If any of you were thinking
about learning Japanese,

00:49:00.880 --> 00:49:03.130 align:middle line:84%
this is one of the things
you will have to learn.

00:49:03.130 --> 00:49:04.948 align:middle line:84%
I'll feel bad if,
now, any of you

00:49:04.948 --> 00:49:06.490 align:middle line:84%
were thinking about
learning Japanese

00:49:06.490 --> 00:49:07.900 align:middle line:90%
but have now changed your mind.

00:49:07.900 --> 00:49:09.220 align:middle line:90%
It's not that bad, really.

00:49:09.220 --> 00:49:11.905 align:middle line:90%


00:49:11.905 --> 00:49:13.280 align:middle line:84%
And then there
are minimal pairs.

00:49:13.280 --> 00:49:17.060 align:middle line:84%
So "rain" in Tokyo
Japanese is "ame."

00:49:17.060 --> 00:49:21.230 align:middle line:84%
"Candy" in Tokyo
Japanese is "ame,"

00:49:21.230 --> 00:49:23.810 align:middle line:84%
so they have the same
consonants and vowels in them

00:49:23.810 --> 00:49:26.750 align:middle line:84%
in the same sequence, but
different positions of pitch

00:49:26.750 --> 00:49:27.380 align:middle line:90%
accent.

00:49:27.380 --> 00:49:29.630 align:middle line:84%
"Candy" is unaccented
and "rain" has an accent

00:49:29.630 --> 00:49:30.590 align:middle line:90%
on the first syllable.

00:49:30.590 --> 00:49:32.300 align:middle line:84%
Or the one that
everybody always uses

00:49:32.300 --> 00:49:34.370 align:middle line:84%
is "chopsticks," which
is HAshi, "bridge,"

00:49:34.370 --> 00:49:40.700 align:middle line:84%
which is "haSHI," and
"edge," which is "haSHI."

00:49:40.700 --> 00:49:42.170 align:middle line:84%
"Bridge" and "edge,"
there's a lot

00:49:42.170 --> 00:49:44.420 align:middle line:84%
of beautiful Japanese
phonology trying to figure out

00:49:44.420 --> 00:49:46.712 align:middle line:84%
whether there's a difference
between the pronunciations

00:49:46.712 --> 00:49:47.712 align:middle line:90%
of "bridge" and "edge."

00:49:47.712 --> 00:49:49.670 align:middle line:84%
The place where everybody
can hear a difference

00:49:49.670 --> 00:49:51.120 align:middle line:90%
is when you add suffixes.

00:49:51.120 --> 00:49:55.310 align:middle line:84%
So if you add, say, the
nominative suffix, "-ga,"

00:49:55.310 --> 00:49:59.840 align:middle line:90%
to those, "edge" is like fish.

00:49:59.840 --> 00:50:03.840 align:middle line:84%
So not only will edge be
low, high, but then the "-ga"

00:50:03.840 --> 00:50:06.440 align:middle line:84%
will also be high,
so you get "haSHIGA."

00:50:06.440 --> 00:50:11.390 align:middle line:84%
Whereas "bridge" has an accent,
that means there'll be a high

00:50:11.390 --> 00:50:13.970 align:middle line:84%
on the "-shi," but then
it'll be low from then on.

00:50:13.970 --> 00:50:18.200 align:middle line:84%
So "edge," the nominative
is "haSHIGA," but "bridge,"

00:50:18.200 --> 00:50:21.240 align:middle line:84%
the nominative is
"haSHIga," low, high, low.

00:50:21.240 --> 00:50:23.570 align:middle line:84%
So all fun things
to look forward

00:50:23.570 --> 00:50:26.270 align:middle line:84%
to if you decide to
try to learn Japanese.

00:50:26.270 --> 00:50:28.670 align:middle line:90%
Now here's a generalization.

00:50:28.670 --> 00:50:32.180 align:middle line:84%
And remember, I told you
when we were doing Yawelmani

00:50:32.180 --> 00:50:34.370 align:middle line:84%
and also when we
were doing English,

00:50:34.370 --> 00:50:37.010 align:middle line:84%
we're going to talk about
various kinds of things

00:50:37.010 --> 00:50:39.470 align:middle line:84%
this language does under
different circumstances.

00:50:39.470 --> 00:50:42.560 align:middle line:84%
But here's a generalization
that holds in Japanese,

00:50:42.560 --> 00:50:43.880 align:middle line:90%
at least Tokyo Japanese.

00:50:43.880 --> 00:50:46.910 align:middle line:90%
Words have at most one accent.

00:50:46.910 --> 00:50:48.950 align:middle line:84%
They don't have more
than one accent.

00:50:48.950 --> 00:50:50.720 align:middle line:84%
And so we're going to
look at cases where

00:50:50.720 --> 00:50:53.570 align:middle line:84%
words are going to
experience a temptation

00:50:53.570 --> 00:50:55.220 align:middle line:90%
to have more than one accent.

00:50:55.220 --> 00:50:56.990 align:middle line:84%
And they're going
to do various things

00:50:56.990 --> 00:51:00.495 align:middle line:84%
to avoid that, various
things, kind of like Yawelmani

00:51:00.495 --> 00:51:02.120 align:middle line:84%
was experiencing the
temptation to have

00:51:02.120 --> 00:51:05.450 align:middle line:84%
three consonants in a row, and
it saved itself in various ways

00:51:05.450 --> 00:51:07.005 align:middle line:90%
under different circumstances.

00:51:07.005 --> 00:51:08.880 align:middle line:84%
We're going to see the
same thing in Japanese

00:51:08.880 --> 00:51:10.463 align:middle line:84%
and the moral is
going to be the same.

00:51:10.463 --> 00:51:13.250 align:middle line:84%
Here's a language that
has a bumper sticker.

00:51:13.250 --> 00:51:15.740 align:middle line:84%
It has an inspirational
poster on its wall.

00:51:15.740 --> 00:51:18.470 align:middle line:84%
Don't have more than one
accent in your words.

00:51:18.470 --> 00:51:21.680 align:middle line:84%
There's a cute picture of a
kitten or something under it.

00:51:21.680 --> 00:51:24.140 align:middle line:84%
And then the way it
achieves that is going

00:51:24.140 --> 00:51:25.340 align:middle line:90%
to vary from word to word.

00:51:25.340 --> 00:51:28.490 align:middle line:84%
That's going to be the moral
of what we're going to see.

00:51:28.490 --> 00:51:29.450 align:middle line:90%
Let's look at that.

00:51:29.450 --> 00:51:32.790 align:middle line:90%


00:51:32.790 --> 00:51:36.030 align:middle line:84%
There's "pillow," "pillow" has
accent on the first syllable.

00:51:36.030 --> 00:51:39.660 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so "from the pillow" is
something like, "MAkura-kara,"

00:51:39.660 --> 00:51:44.160 align:middle line:84%
and "to the pillow" is
something like, "MAkura-made."

00:51:44.160 --> 00:51:49.240 align:middle line:84%
The heart, the head,
yeah, same kinds of deal.

00:51:49.240 --> 00:51:52.140 align:middle line:84%
So "from the heart"
is "koKOro-kara,"

00:51:52.140 --> 00:51:54.810 align:middle line:90%
with accent just on "koKOro."

00:51:54.810 --> 00:51:58.215 align:middle line:84%
To the heart is "koKoro-made,"
with accent just on the "-ko."

00:51:58.215 --> 00:52:01.980 align:middle line:90%


00:52:01.980 --> 00:52:04.020 align:middle line:84%
"From the head" and "to
the head," same deal.

00:52:04.020 --> 00:52:07.080 align:middle line:84%
There's an accent on the
last syllable of "head."

00:52:07.080 --> 00:52:09.300 align:middle line:84%
But when we look at
"fish," and remember

00:52:09.300 --> 00:52:11.370 align:middle line:84%
that fish is
underlyingly unaccented,

00:52:11.370 --> 00:52:13.980 align:middle line:84%
that's why there aren't
any accented vowels there

00:52:13.980 --> 00:52:16.230 align:middle line:84%
in "sakana," the
word for "fish."

00:52:16.230 --> 00:52:17.955 align:middle line:84%
When we look at
"fish," we look at it

00:52:17.955 --> 00:52:20.880 align:middle line:84%
and we find a difference
between "from" and "to."

00:52:20.880 --> 00:52:25.650 align:middle line:84%
So for "from the fish," the
whole sequence, "sakana-kara,"

00:52:25.650 --> 00:52:27.330 align:middle line:90%
is unaccented.

00:52:27.330 --> 00:52:31.350 align:middle line:84%
But for "to the fish,"
the sequence "-made"

00:52:31.350 --> 00:52:32.670 align:middle line:90%
has an accent on "-made."

00:52:32.670 --> 00:52:37.960 align:middle line:84%
It's "sakana-MAde," with
a dip at the end there.

00:52:37.960 --> 00:52:42.120 align:middle line:84%
So "from" and "to" act the
same for "pillow," "heart,"

00:52:42.120 --> 00:52:43.950 align:middle line:90%
and "head," the accented nouns.

00:52:43.950 --> 00:52:45.960 align:middle line:84%
But for the unaccented
noun, the word

00:52:45.960 --> 00:52:48.840 align:middle line:84%
for "fish," which doesn't
have an accent on its own,

00:52:48.840 --> 00:52:51.030 align:middle line:84%
then we see a difference
between "from" and "to."

00:52:51.030 --> 00:52:54.420 align:middle line:84%
"From" doesn't have an
accent but "to" acts like it

00:52:54.420 --> 00:52:55.740 align:middle line:90%
has an accent all of a sudden.

00:52:55.740 --> 00:52:58.500 align:middle line:90%


00:52:58.500 --> 00:53:01.200 align:middle line:84%
A conventional way of
talking about what's going on

00:53:01.200 --> 00:53:07.050 align:middle line:84%
says, yeah, there are,
what, four, or five-- six!

00:53:07.050 --> 00:53:09.210 align:middle line:84%
There are six morphemes
in this slide.

00:53:09.210 --> 00:53:12.150 align:middle line:84%
There's "pillow," "heart,"
"head," "fish," "from," and

00:53:12.150 --> 00:53:13.650 align:middle line:90%
"to."

00:53:13.650 --> 00:53:15.960 align:middle line:84%
"Pillow," "heart," and
"head" are accented.

00:53:15.960 --> 00:53:17.520 align:middle line:90%
"Fish" is unaccented.

00:53:17.520 --> 00:53:18.450 align:middle line:90%
We already knew that.

00:53:18.450 --> 00:53:20.730 align:middle line:84%
I showed you that on
the earlier slide.

00:53:20.730 --> 00:53:22.470 align:middle line:90%
"From" is also unaccented.

00:53:22.470 --> 00:53:27.180 align:middle line:84%
"From" is like "fish,"
but "to" is accented,

00:53:27.180 --> 00:53:31.080 align:middle line:84%
and that's why it's showing up
as accented in that phrase that

00:53:31.080 --> 00:53:35.600 align:middle line:84%
means "to the fish,"
"sakana-MAde."

00:53:35.600 --> 00:53:39.095 align:middle line:84%
"Made" doesn't just mean
"to," it means "as far as."

00:53:39.095 --> 00:53:41.960 align:middle line:84%
It suggests that someone
is crawling on the beach

00:53:41.960 --> 00:53:43.040 align:middle line:90%
up to the fish.

00:53:43.040 --> 00:53:44.480 align:middle line:90%
They got to the fish.

00:53:44.480 --> 00:53:49.030 align:middle line:84%
They got that far,
"sakana-MAde."

00:53:49.030 --> 00:53:55.480 align:middle line:84%
So there are six morphemes
on this slide and four

00:53:55.480 --> 00:54:00.340 align:middle line:84%
of them are accented, "pillow,"
"heart," "head," and "to."

00:54:00.340 --> 00:54:03.610 align:middle line:84%
So we only get to see that "to"
is accented when we combine it

00:54:03.610 --> 00:54:07.390 align:middle line:84%
with an unaccented noun, because
there's this general principle

00:54:07.390 --> 00:54:11.060 align:middle line:84%
that you can't have a word that
has more than one accent in it.

00:54:11.060 --> 00:54:14.650 align:middle line:84%
So when you add to to
a noun that's accented,

00:54:14.650 --> 00:54:16.700 align:middle line:90%
to loses its accent.

00:54:16.700 --> 00:54:18.090 align:middle line:84%
"To" and "from"
act the same way.

00:54:18.090 --> 00:54:19.040 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:54:19.040 --> 00:54:21.420 align:middle line:84%
So here's case number one
of Japanese working out

00:54:21.420 --> 00:54:23.170 align:middle line:84%
a way to make sure
that a single word only

00:54:23.170 --> 00:54:25.400 align:middle line:90%
has a single accent in it.

00:54:25.400 --> 00:54:26.740 align:middle line:90%
Here's a repair that it takes.

00:54:26.740 --> 00:54:30.690 align:middle line:84%
It deletes the accent on "to,"
on the word for "to," "-made."

00:54:30.690 --> 00:54:32.440 align:middle line:84%
There are other repairs
you could imagine.

00:54:32.440 --> 00:54:35.690 align:middle line:84%
It could have gotten rid of
the accent on the noun instead,

00:54:35.690 --> 00:54:36.360 align:middle line:90%
but it doesn't.

00:54:36.360 --> 00:54:37.360 align:middle line:90%
That's not what it does.

00:54:37.360 --> 00:54:40.840 align:middle line:90%


00:54:40.840 --> 00:54:45.430 align:middle line:84%
"Fish," "pillow," here's
another Japanese word, "even,"

00:54:45.430 --> 00:54:49.240 align:middle line:90%
as in "even a fish."

00:54:49.240 --> 00:54:52.150 align:middle line:84%
Try to imagine a
circumstance in which you

00:54:52.150 --> 00:54:56.350 align:middle line:84%
would want to say, "even a
fish," or "even a pillow."

00:54:56.350 --> 00:54:58.480 align:middle line:90%
"Even" is accented.

00:54:58.480 --> 00:55:01.870 align:middle line:84%
We saw that "fish" is unaccented
and "pillow" is accented.

00:55:01.870 --> 00:55:03.730 align:middle line:84%
But when you add
this word for even,

00:55:03.730 --> 00:55:08.140 align:middle line:84%
"-gurai,' both of those
nouns become unaccented.

00:55:08.140 --> 00:55:14.560 align:middle line:84%
So it's sakana-GUrai, and
it's makura-GUrai, even

00:55:14.560 --> 00:55:18.010 align:middle line:90%
though it's MAkura and saKANA.

00:55:18.010 --> 00:55:21.770 align:middle line:84%
So "pillow" is accented on
its first syllable normally,

00:55:21.770 --> 00:55:25.230 align:middle line:84%
but it loses its accent if
you add this "even" thing.

00:55:25.230 --> 00:55:27.690 align:middle line:84%
That's the repair that I
just alluded to accidentally.

00:55:27.690 --> 00:55:31.470 align:middle line:84%
So "even" is like "to"
in that it has an accent.

00:55:31.470 --> 00:55:35.670 align:middle line:84%
So if you were going to
add "even" to these nouns,

00:55:35.670 --> 00:55:38.520 align:middle line:84%
with "fish," no problem, with
"pillow," there is a problem.

00:55:38.520 --> 00:55:40.380 align:middle line:84%
You're in danger of
having two accents.

00:55:40.380 --> 00:55:42.672 align:middle line:84%
And the repair in this case
is to get rid of the accent

00:55:42.672 --> 00:55:44.130 align:middle line:90%
on "pillow."

00:55:44.130 --> 00:55:46.530 align:middle line:84%
Raquel, did I
bulldoze a question?

00:55:46.530 --> 00:55:49.235 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I had a
somewhat related question

00:55:49.235 --> 00:55:50.610 align:middle line:84%
to the topic in
general, which is

00:55:50.610 --> 00:55:54.040 align:middle line:84%
just you're saying that
languages will be like, I don't

00:55:54.040 --> 00:55:55.000 align:middle line:90%
want this to happen?

00:55:55.000 --> 00:55:57.610 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:55:57.610 --> 00:55:59.440 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Do we observe
times in languages

00:55:59.440 --> 00:56:01.780 align:middle line:84%
where they seem like they
don't want things to happen,

00:56:01.780 --> 00:56:04.360 align:middle line:84%
and they just break
their rules all the time?

00:56:04.360 --> 00:56:06.850 align:middle line:84%
Not like English and spelling,
but something like they

00:56:06.850 --> 00:56:09.058 align:middle line:84%
have certain things they
definitely don't want to do,

00:56:09.058 --> 00:56:11.820 align:middle line:84%
so you get just lots and lots of
examples of times [INAUDIBLE]??

00:56:11.820 --> 00:56:14.130 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Of
apparent counterexamples?

00:56:14.130 --> 00:56:17.070 align:middle line:84%
Well, we saw something
sort of like that

00:56:17.070 --> 00:56:20.400 align:middle line:90%
when we were looking at Lardil.

00:56:20.400 --> 00:56:25.230 align:middle line:84%
Maybe you remember,
I showed you that it

00:56:25.230 --> 00:56:31.380 align:middle line:84%
was useful to think
that nouns don't

00:56:31.380 --> 00:56:34.920 align:middle line:90%
like to end in the vowel "u."

00:56:34.920 --> 00:56:37.050 align:middle line:90%
The vowel "u" changes to "a."

00:56:37.050 --> 00:56:40.390 align:middle line:84%
So there was an
underlying [NON-ENGLISH],,

00:56:40.390 --> 00:56:44.190 align:middle line:84%
which means "blood," which you
get to see in the accusative,

00:56:44.190 --> 00:56:45.490 align:middle line:90%
which is [NON-ENGLISH].

00:56:45.490 --> 00:56:48.310 align:middle line:84%
But the nominative
is [NON-ENGLISH]..

00:56:48.310 --> 00:56:50.700 align:middle line:84%
And we said, well,
that's because Lardil

00:56:50.700 --> 00:56:52.140 align:middle line:84%
doesn't like words
to end in "u."

00:56:52.140 --> 00:56:54.180 align:middle line:90%
it changes the "u" to an "a."

00:56:54.180 --> 00:56:56.910 align:middle line:84%
Lardil also doesn't like
words to end in "k."

00:56:56.910 --> 00:56:59.130 align:middle line:84%
So there's an underlying
word for "boomerang,"

00:56:59.130 --> 00:57:01.020 align:middle line:84%
which is underlyingly
[NON-ENGLISH],,

00:57:01.020 --> 00:57:02.430 align:middle line:90%
which you see in the accusative.

00:57:02.430 --> 00:57:06.100 align:middle line:84%
It's [NON-ENGLISH],, but the
nominative is [NON-ENGLISH]..

00:57:06.100 --> 00:57:09.780 align:middle line:84%
And so we said, yeah, it doesn't
like words to end in "k."

00:57:09.780 --> 00:57:13.830 align:middle line:84%
But Lardil also has words
that are underlyingly

00:57:13.830 --> 00:57:15.810 align:middle line:90%
ending in a "u" and then a "k."

00:57:15.810 --> 00:57:17.580 align:middle line:90%
So this is the word for "story."

00:57:17.580 --> 00:57:21.630 align:middle line:84%
The accusative is [NON-ENGLISH],,
but the nominative is

00:57:21.630 --> 00:57:22.555 align:middle line:90%
[NON-ENGLISH].

00:57:22.555 --> 00:57:24.713 align:middle line:84%
So you get rid of
the "k" at the end,

00:57:24.713 --> 00:57:26.130 align:middle line:84%
but now what you've
got at the end

00:57:26.130 --> 00:57:29.070 align:middle line:84%
is an "u," which we said
when we looked at "blood,"

00:57:29.070 --> 00:57:30.480 align:middle line:90%
Lardil doesn't like.

00:57:30.480 --> 00:57:33.240 align:middle line:84%
Is that the kind of thing
you're talking about?

00:57:33.240 --> 00:57:33.930 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:57:33.930 --> 00:57:37.900 align:middle line:84%
So just is it actually
common that languages

00:57:37.900 --> 00:57:39.460 align:middle line:84%
will break these
important rules,

00:57:39.460 --> 00:57:42.535 align:middle line:84%
or is it actually
pretty infrequent?

00:57:42.535 --> 00:57:44.160 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So
we're going to see,

00:57:44.160 --> 00:57:45.940 align:middle line:84%
I think it's fair to
say that it's common.

00:57:45.940 --> 00:57:48.965 align:middle line:84%
So I'm deliberately
finding some cases,

00:57:48.965 --> 00:57:50.340 align:middle line:84%
it may not feel
this way, but I'm

00:57:50.340 --> 00:57:53.220 align:middle line:84%
finding some cases that are
comparatively simple, where

00:57:53.220 --> 00:57:58.120 align:middle line:84%
there is a single principle that
a language is trying to achieve

00:57:58.120 --> 00:57:59.790 align:middle line:84%
and it achieves it
in various ways.

00:57:59.790 --> 00:58:02.790 align:middle line:84%
That's why we're talking
about this kind of case.

00:58:02.790 --> 00:58:06.840 align:middle line:84%
But there isn't any conflict
between the various things

00:58:06.840 --> 00:58:08.590 align:middle line:90%
that a language wants to do.

00:58:08.590 --> 00:58:11.310 align:middle line:84%
So we're talking about cases
where these languages only

00:58:11.310 --> 00:58:14.480 align:middle line:84%
have one thing they want to
do and they always achieve it.

00:58:14.480 --> 00:58:16.980 align:middle line:84%
There are cases where a language
has multiple things that it

00:58:16.980 --> 00:58:19.360 align:middle line:84%
wants to do and they're not
compatible with each other,

00:58:19.360 --> 00:58:20.820 align:middle line:90%
and the language has to choose.

00:58:20.820 --> 00:58:22.440 align:middle line:84%
So you can convince
yourself that it

00:58:22.440 --> 00:58:24.130 align:middle line:84%
wants to do both
of these things,

00:58:24.130 --> 00:58:27.480 align:middle line:84%
but sometimes it just has
to give one of them up.

00:58:27.480 --> 00:58:29.460 align:middle line:84%
And Lardil might be
a case like that.

00:58:29.460 --> 00:58:31.150 align:middle line:84%
That's a kind of
thing that happens.

00:58:31.150 --> 00:58:33.600 align:middle line:84%
So what we'll end up with is
a picture in which languages

00:58:33.600 --> 00:58:36.060 align:middle line:84%
might have many things
that they want to do,

00:58:36.060 --> 00:58:40.170 align:middle line:84%
but they have a
list of priorities.

00:58:40.170 --> 00:58:42.600 align:middle line:84%
And we're talking in these
cases about languages that--

00:58:42.600 --> 00:58:44.850 align:middle line:84%
we're only talking about
priority number one.

00:58:44.850 --> 00:58:45.870 align:middle line:90%
It always wins.

00:58:45.870 --> 00:58:48.032 align:middle line:84%
You always do whatever you
have to to achieve that.

00:58:48.032 --> 00:58:50.490 align:middle line:84%
But there are also languages
that have priority number one,

00:58:50.490 --> 00:58:52.860 align:middle line:84%
and number two, and number
three, and number four.

00:58:52.860 --> 00:58:55.110 align:middle line:90%
We find examples like that, too.

00:58:55.110 --> 00:58:56.565 align:middle line:90%
Does that answer your question?

00:58:56.565 --> 00:58:57.370 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:58:57.370 --> 00:58:58.790 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Cool.

00:58:58.790 --> 00:59:00.410 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:59:00.410 --> 00:59:01.946 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:59:01.946 --> 00:59:05.790 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So taking the
case to a study of English.

00:59:05.790 --> 00:59:09.825 align:middle line:84%
I would assume that
no double stridents

00:59:09.825 --> 00:59:10.912 align:middle line:90%
is very high priority.

00:59:10.912 --> 00:59:11.787 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes.

00:59:11.787 --> 00:59:13.070 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Because
I've never heard it.

00:59:13.070 --> 00:59:15.195 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: We don't
have any words like that.

00:59:15.195 --> 00:59:16.283 align:middle line:90%
Right.

00:59:16.283 --> 00:59:17.450 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's a good example.

00:59:17.450 --> 00:59:19.550 align:middle line:84%
So that's a case
where English just

00:59:19.550 --> 00:59:22.460 align:middle line:84%
will not tolerate cases where
a word ends in two stridents.

00:59:22.460 --> 00:59:24.110 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, we don't have
words like that.

00:59:24.110 --> 00:59:25.850 align:middle line:90%
Good example.

00:59:25.850 --> 00:59:27.150 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:59:27.150 --> 00:59:27.650 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:59:27.650 --> 00:59:33.120 align:middle line:84%
Let me continue crunching
through Japanese here.

00:59:33.120 --> 00:59:35.660 align:middle line:84%
So again, Japanese
doesn't allow for words

00:59:35.660 --> 00:59:36.890 align:middle line:90%
with more than one accent.

00:59:36.890 --> 00:59:40.430 align:middle line:84%
We've seen that "-made," this
word for "to," loses its accent

00:59:40.430 --> 00:59:41.468 align:middle line:90%
after an accented word.

00:59:41.468 --> 00:59:43.760 align:middle line:84%
And now we're seeing that if
you have an accented word,

00:59:43.760 --> 00:59:47.180 align:middle line:84%
it will lose its
accent before "-gurai."

00:59:47.180 --> 00:59:50.870 align:middle line:84%
So again, Japanese has
this general thing,

00:59:50.870 --> 00:59:53.400 align:middle line:84%
very high priority, no words
with more than one accent

00:59:53.400 --> 00:59:53.900 align:middle line:90%
in them.

00:59:53.900 --> 00:59:55.687 align:middle line:84%
And then it has
various fixes depending

00:59:55.687 --> 00:59:57.020 align:middle line:90%
on the particular circumstances.

00:59:57.020 --> 00:59:58.520 align:middle line:90%
We've talked about two so far.

00:59:58.520 --> 01:00:01.370 align:middle line:90%
We'll see some more.

01:00:01.370 --> 01:00:01.870 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:00:01.870 --> 01:00:06.250 align:middle line:84%
So Japanese avoids having more
than one accent in this unit

01:00:06.250 --> 01:00:08.590 align:middle line:84%
consisting of a word
along with its suffixes

01:00:08.590 --> 01:00:09.507 align:middle line:90%
through various means.

01:00:09.507 --> 01:00:11.715 align:middle line:84%
You either get rid of the
first accent or the second,

01:00:11.715 --> 01:00:13.330 align:middle line:84%
depending on which
accent exactly it

01:00:13.330 --> 01:00:14.680 align:middle line:90%
is that you're talking about.

01:00:14.680 --> 01:00:17.222 align:middle line:84%
We can maybe think of this as
an example of the kind of thing

01:00:17.222 --> 01:00:21.250 align:middle line:84%
that [INAUDIBLE]
Japanese's highest

01:00:21.250 --> 01:00:25.000 align:middle line:84%
priority is, don't have a word
with more than one accent.

01:00:25.000 --> 01:00:26.740 align:middle line:84%
And then it has these
other priorities,

01:00:26.740 --> 01:00:29.020 align:middle line:84%
like keep the
accent on the noun,

01:00:29.020 --> 01:00:33.070 align:middle line:84%
and keep the accent on "-made,"
the word for "to," and keep

01:00:33.070 --> 01:00:35.680 align:middle line:84%
the accent on "-gurai,"
the word for "even."

01:00:35.680 --> 01:00:38.230 align:middle line:84%
And what we're seeing
maybe is that we

01:00:38.230 --> 01:00:43.180 align:middle line:84%
should list the priorities
as only one accent.

01:00:43.180 --> 01:00:45.880 align:middle line:90%
That's number one.

01:00:45.880 --> 01:00:49.990 align:middle line:84%
And number two is keep
the accent on "-gurai,"

01:00:49.990 --> 01:00:51.550 align:middle line:90%
this word that means "even."

01:00:51.550 --> 01:00:56.650 align:middle line:84%
And number three is,
keep the accent on nouns.

01:00:56.650 --> 01:01:04.150 align:middle line:84%
And then, number four is,
keep the accent on "-made,"

01:01:04.150 --> 01:01:06.610 align:middle line:90%
this word for "to."

01:01:06.610 --> 01:01:08.620 align:middle line:84%
And these priorities
we should think

01:01:08.620 --> 01:01:10.340 align:middle line:90%
of as being in this order.

01:01:10.340 --> 01:01:12.220 align:middle line:84%
So Japanese really
only wants one accent

01:01:12.220 --> 01:01:14.840 align:middle line:84%
per word, that's the
most important thing.

01:01:14.840 --> 01:01:18.460 align:middle line:84%
And then also, it really wants
to keep the accent on "-gurai."

01:01:18.460 --> 01:01:20.650 align:middle line:84%
It would like to keep
the accent on nouns.

01:01:20.650 --> 01:01:23.240 align:middle line:84%
So if it just has to choose
between the accent on a noun

01:01:23.240 --> 01:01:25.490 align:middle line:84%
and the accent on "-made,"
well, it'll keep the accent

01:01:25.490 --> 01:01:26.320 align:middle line:90%
on the noun.

01:01:26.320 --> 01:01:28.403 align:middle line:90%
It'll lose the accent on "to."

01:01:28.403 --> 01:01:31.070 align:middle line:84%
But if it's choosing between the
accent on a noun and the accent

01:01:31.070 --> 01:01:33.070 align:middle line:84%
on "-gurai," on even,
well, it'll choose the one

01:01:33.070 --> 01:01:34.600 align:middle line:90%
on "-gurai."

01:01:34.600 --> 01:01:36.730 align:middle line:84%
So it's as though
we have, again, this

01:01:36.730 --> 01:01:38.650 align:middle line:84%
ranked list of
priorities, these things

01:01:38.650 --> 01:01:40.780 align:middle line:90%
that Japanese cares about.

01:01:40.780 --> 01:01:43.480 align:middle line:84%
And this sort of way of
thinking about things

01:01:43.480 --> 01:01:45.910 align:middle line:84%
raises all kinds of questions,
like, what kinds of things

01:01:45.910 --> 01:01:47.980 align:middle line:84%
do languages get to
care about, and why?

01:01:47.980 --> 01:01:50.350 align:middle line:84%
And what determines which
things are more important

01:01:50.350 --> 01:01:51.350 align:middle line:90%
than which other things?

01:01:51.350 --> 01:01:52.850 align:middle line:84%
Is there anything
to say about that?

01:01:52.850 --> 01:01:55.090 align:middle line:84%
These are all open
topics in phonology,

01:01:55.090 --> 01:01:56.620 align:middle line:90%
things people work on.

01:01:56.620 --> 01:02:02.700 align:middle line:90%


01:02:02.700 --> 01:02:04.997 align:middle line:84%
I'll show you a few more
facts about Japanese

01:02:04.997 --> 01:02:05.955 align:middle line:90%
before we have to stop.

01:02:05.955 --> 01:02:08.640 align:middle line:90%


01:02:08.640 --> 01:02:10.960 align:middle line:84%
Here are some
Japanese compounds.

01:02:10.960 --> 01:02:14.380 align:middle line:84%
So compounds where you take two
nouns and put them together.

01:02:14.380 --> 01:02:20.190 align:middle line:84%
And of course, you may all be
imagining potential problems,

01:02:20.190 --> 01:02:22.950 align:middle line:84%
because as you know,
a single word can only

01:02:22.950 --> 01:02:24.135 align:middle line:90%
have one accent in it.

01:02:24.135 --> 01:02:27.330 align:middle line:90%


01:02:27.330 --> 01:02:31.260 align:middle line:84%
What's striking about compounds
is that the compound word,

01:02:31.260 --> 01:02:35.190 align:middle line:84%
the word that you create when
you put two words together,

01:02:35.190 --> 01:02:37.380 align:middle line:84%
indeed, it can only
have one accent in it.

01:02:37.380 --> 01:02:39.360 align:middle line:84%
But the accent it
has in it doesn't

01:02:39.360 --> 01:02:43.330 align:middle line:84%
have to be the accent of
either of the two components.

01:02:43.330 --> 01:02:46.330 align:middle line:84%
So take the last
example, there's a place.

01:02:46.330 --> 01:02:51.180 align:middle line:84%
It's a place I lived in Japan
for about a year, Chiba.

01:02:51.180 --> 01:02:51.900 align:middle line:90%
Chiba ken.

01:02:51.900 --> 01:02:54.510 align:middle line:84%
So Japan is divided into
what are called prefectures.

01:02:54.510 --> 01:02:57.630 align:middle line:84%
They're like states, so there
are a certain number of them

01:02:57.630 --> 01:02:59.700 align:middle line:90%
in Japan.

01:02:59.700 --> 01:03:01.800 align:middle line:84%
There's a city that's
the capital of Chiba

01:03:01.800 --> 01:03:03.780 align:middle line:90%
prefecture, Chiba City.

01:03:03.780 --> 01:03:08.222 align:middle line:84%
If you've ever read any William
Gibson, you've read about it.

01:03:08.222 --> 01:03:09.180 align:middle line:90%
That's science fiction.

01:03:09.180 --> 01:03:11.680 align:middle line:90%
Chiba is not like that.

01:03:11.680 --> 01:03:15.630 align:middle line:84%
The place itself has accent
on the first syllable,

01:03:15.630 --> 01:03:17.790 align:middle line:90%
so it's "CHIba."

01:03:17.790 --> 01:03:22.500 align:middle line:84%
And the word for "prefecture"
has an accent on it,

01:03:22.500 --> 01:03:23.640 align:middle line:90%
it's "ken."

01:03:23.640 --> 01:03:27.598 align:middle line:84%
But the compound, "Chiba
prefecture," has one accent.

01:03:27.598 --> 01:03:29.640 align:middle line:84%
That's sort of what you
expect, words should only

01:03:29.640 --> 01:03:32.190 align:middle line:84%
have one accent, but it
is on the one syllable

01:03:32.190 --> 01:03:33.900 align:middle line:84%
that it is not
accented ordinarily.

01:03:33.900 --> 01:03:35.760 align:middle line:90%
It's "chiBA ken."

01:03:35.760 --> 01:03:37.020 align:middle line:90%
Sorry, "chiBA ken."

01:03:37.020 --> 01:03:39.120 align:middle line:84%
That's right, "chiBA
ken," so accent

01:03:39.120 --> 01:03:42.210 align:middle line:84%
just on the second
syllable, not the syllable

01:03:42.210 --> 01:03:44.005 align:middle line:84%
that's ordinarily
accented in "Chiba,"

01:03:44.005 --> 01:03:46.380 align:middle line:84%
and not the syllable that's
ordinarily accented in "ken,"

01:03:46.380 --> 01:03:47.070 align:middle line:90%
the other one.

01:03:47.070 --> 01:03:49.770 align:middle line:90%


01:03:49.770 --> 01:03:51.570 align:middle line:84%
You also get accent
in compounds.

01:03:51.570 --> 01:03:53.730 align:middle line:84%
You can see in
the first example,

01:03:53.730 --> 01:03:57.570 align:middle line:84%
you get accent in compounds
even if the components

01:03:57.570 --> 01:04:00.390 align:middle line:84%
of the compound are not
accented themselves.

01:04:00.390 --> 01:04:04.410 align:middle line:84%
So a "milk drinking
child," "milk drinking"

01:04:04.410 --> 01:04:06.870 align:middle line:84%
is itself a compound, but
anyway, "milk drinking"

01:04:06.870 --> 01:04:07.680 align:middle line:90%
is not accented.

01:04:07.680 --> 01:04:10.680 align:middle line:84%
It's "tinomi," and "child"
is not accented, it's "ko."

01:04:10.680 --> 01:04:14.880 align:middle line:84%
But the compound has an accent,
which is on the "i" there,

01:04:14.880 --> 01:04:17.640 align:middle line:90%
so it's "tinoMIko."

01:04:17.640 --> 01:04:19.800 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so a nursing baby,
"milk drinking child."

01:04:19.800 --> 01:04:22.540 align:middle line:90%


01:04:22.540 --> 01:04:24.580 align:middle line:84%
Which prefecture
is your mom from?

01:04:24.580 --> 01:04:25.495 align:middle line:90%
Do you know?

01:04:25.495 --> 01:04:26.620 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: She's from Tokyo.

01:04:26.620 --> 01:04:28.037 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
She's from Tokyo?

01:04:28.037 --> 01:04:30.050 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

01:04:30.050 --> 01:04:32.600 align:middle line:90%
Great city, Tokyo.

01:04:32.600 --> 01:04:35.478 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, so all of
these compounds,

01:04:35.478 --> 01:04:37.520 align:middle line:84%
they have one accent in
them, exactly one accent.

01:04:37.520 --> 01:04:39.140 align:middle line:84%
And the accent is
not necessarily

01:04:39.140 --> 01:04:41.850 align:middle line:84%
the accent of either
of the components.

01:04:41.850 --> 01:04:43.520 align:middle line:84%
In fact, I think
I've carefully set it

01:04:43.520 --> 01:04:47.210 align:middle line:84%
up so that it isn't in
any of these examples.

01:04:47.210 --> 01:04:50.390 align:middle line:84%
Where is the accent
going in these compounds?

01:04:50.390 --> 01:04:51.590 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

01:04:51.590 --> 01:04:53.190 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The second
to last syllable.

01:04:53.190 --> 01:04:55.023 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So
it's going on the second

01:04:55.023 --> 01:04:56.840 align:middle line:84%
to last syllable in
all of these examples.

01:04:56.840 --> 01:04:57.927 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

01:04:57.927 --> 01:04:59.510 align:middle line:84%
Is there another way
you could say it?

01:04:59.510 --> 01:05:00.190 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

01:05:00.190 --> 01:05:02.398 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But would it be,
like, on the last syllable

01:05:02.398 --> 01:05:04.050 align:middle line:84%
of the first part
of the compound?

01:05:04.050 --> 01:05:05.060 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: It's
going on the last syllable

01:05:05.060 --> 01:05:06.530 align:middle line:84%
of the first part
of the compound.

01:05:06.530 --> 01:05:08.090 align:middle line:84%
I haven't given you
any examples here yet

01:05:08.090 --> 01:05:09.965 align:middle line:84%
that distinguish those
two theories but those

01:05:09.965 --> 01:05:13.125 align:middle line:84%
are both perfectly good
theories, what's going on.

01:05:13.125 --> 01:05:13.625 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:05:13.625 --> 01:05:17.690 align:middle line:90%


01:05:17.690 --> 01:05:21.930 align:middle line:84%
Here are some places
to distinguish them.

01:05:21.930 --> 01:05:23.880 align:middle line:90%
I especially like the last one.

01:05:23.880 --> 01:05:28.230 align:middle line:84%
So the Japanese word for "fried
potato," or for french fries--

01:05:28.230 --> 01:05:30.480 align:middle line:90%
"furaido poteto," fried potato.

01:05:30.480 --> 01:05:36.540 align:middle line:90%


01:05:36.540 --> 01:05:38.430 align:middle line:84%
Well, what we can
see actually is

01:05:38.430 --> 01:05:42.210 align:middle line:84%
that neither of those
theories is right.

01:05:42.210 --> 01:05:45.870 align:middle line:84%
If we ignore "nursing baby"
and "Kagawa prefecture,"

01:05:45.870 --> 01:05:49.710 align:middle line:84%
where is accent going in
"raw egg," and "field mouse,"

01:05:49.710 --> 01:05:50.565 align:middle line:90%
and "fried potato"?

01:05:50.565 --> 01:05:54.670 align:middle line:90%


01:05:54.670 --> 01:05:55.300 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

01:05:55.300 --> 01:05:57.750 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Isn't it called
the antepenultimate?

01:05:57.750 --> 01:06:00.000 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, it is
called the antepenultimate,

01:06:00.000 --> 01:06:01.500 align:middle line:90%
yes.

01:06:01.500 --> 01:06:03.240 align:middle line:84%
That is true in all
of these examples.

01:06:03.240 --> 01:06:05.240 align:middle line:84%
It's going on the
antepenultimate syllable, that

01:06:05.240 --> 01:06:07.770 align:middle line:84%
is, the syllable which is
before the penultimate syllable,

01:06:07.770 --> 01:06:12.630 align:middle line:84%
that is, the syllable which is
three syllables from the end.

01:06:12.630 --> 01:06:14.675 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, that's true
for all of these.

01:06:14.675 --> 01:06:16.050 align:middle line:84%
There's another
way of saying it,

01:06:16.050 --> 01:06:19.720 align:middle line:84%
though, sort of
like there were two

01:06:19.720 --> 01:06:20.970 align:middle line:90%
ways to say it the first time.

01:06:20.970 --> 01:06:21.330 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:06:21.330 --> 01:06:22.650 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The first
syllable of the second.

01:06:22.650 --> 01:06:23.220 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
The first syllable

01:06:23.220 --> 01:06:25.120 align:middle line:84%
of the second part
of the compound.

01:06:25.120 --> 01:06:27.372 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, that works
for all of these.

01:06:27.372 --> 01:06:29.580 align:middle line:84%
I can't remember if I have
any slides that show this,

01:06:29.580 --> 01:06:32.310 align:middle line:84%
but that's actually the
right way to talk about it.

01:06:32.310 --> 01:06:35.670 align:middle line:84%
The right way to talk about
where accent goes in a compound

01:06:35.670 --> 01:06:39.360 align:middle line:90%
is, it goes at--

01:06:39.360 --> 01:06:43.250 align:middle line:84%
so first, it has to
only have one accent.

01:06:43.250 --> 01:06:46.730 align:middle line:84%
The accent has to be next to
the boundary between the words

01:06:46.730 --> 01:06:50.360 align:middle line:84%
and it can't be final, so it
can't be on the last syllable.

01:06:50.360 --> 01:06:53.450 align:middle line:84%
So in words like
"Kagawa prefecture,"

01:06:53.450 --> 01:06:56.300 align:middle line:84%
so when you take "kagawa"
and "ken," what you get

01:06:56.300 --> 01:06:57.800 align:middle line:90%
is "kagawa ken."

01:06:57.800 --> 01:07:01.370 align:middle line:90%


01:07:01.370 --> 01:07:04.100 align:middle line:84%
So there the accent goes
at the end of "kagawa"

01:07:04.100 --> 01:07:06.183 align:middle line:90%
because it can't be final.

01:07:06.183 --> 01:07:07.850 align:middle line:84%
It can't be on the
word for "prefecture"

01:07:07.850 --> 01:07:09.920 align:middle line:84%
because that's only
one syllable long.

01:07:09.920 --> 01:07:13.010 align:middle line:84%
But on "fried potato,"
it can be on "potato,"

01:07:13.010 --> 01:07:17.630 align:middle line:84%
on the first
syllable of "potato"

01:07:17.630 --> 01:07:19.790 align:middle line:84%
because it's still
between the boundary,

01:07:19.790 --> 01:07:22.400 align:middle line:84%
next to the boundary between
the words and it's not final.

01:07:22.400 --> 01:07:24.480 align:middle line:84%
And then, if possible,
that's what you prefer.

01:07:24.480 --> 01:07:30.110 align:middle line:84%
So you prefer "furAIdo
POteto" to "furaiDO poteto,"

01:07:30.110 --> 01:07:34.280 align:middle line:84%
where the accent would be at
the end of the first syllable.

01:07:34.280 --> 01:07:35.930 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

01:07:35.930 --> 01:07:36.560 align:middle line:90%
Make sense?

01:07:36.560 --> 01:07:40.050 align:middle line:90%


01:07:40.050 --> 01:07:45.000 align:middle line:90%
So here's another place where--

01:07:45.000 --> 01:07:48.870 align:middle line:84%
so I did something
like this over here.

01:07:48.870 --> 01:07:51.000 align:middle line:84%
We'll just end with this
and we'll pick it up here

01:07:51.000 --> 01:07:52.590 align:middle line:90%
next time.

01:07:52.590 --> 01:07:55.860 align:middle line:84%
Here's a place where it's useful
to think of Japanese as having

01:07:55.860 --> 01:08:00.580 align:middle line:84%
a bunch of priorities which
it sort of has in an order.

01:08:00.580 --> 01:08:05.040 align:middle line:84%
So if it's starting off
with "kagawa" and "ken,"

01:08:05.040 --> 01:08:09.880 align:middle line:84%
Kagawa prefecture, it has
to decide, what should I do?

01:08:09.880 --> 01:08:12.000 align:middle line:84%
Should I keep both of
the accents I've got?

01:08:12.000 --> 01:08:13.770 align:middle line:84%
Should I just have
one accent, and if I'm

01:08:13.770 --> 01:08:16.200 align:middle line:84%
going to have one accent,
where should it go?

01:08:16.200 --> 01:08:19.500 align:middle line:84%
We've seen already from
other examples in Japanese

01:08:19.500 --> 01:08:23.580 align:middle line:84%
that it has a very high ranking
preference for there only

01:08:23.580 --> 01:08:25.560 align:middle line:90%
to be one accent.

01:08:25.560 --> 01:08:28.020 align:middle line:84%
And so it's not going
to keep two accents.

01:08:28.020 --> 01:08:30.901 align:middle line:84%
That's the first
candidate there,

01:08:30.901 --> 01:08:32.609 align:middle line:84%
the first thing that
it's imagining maybe

01:08:32.609 --> 01:08:34.350 align:middle line:90%
being able to do.

01:08:34.350 --> 01:08:38.279 align:middle line:84%
And then when it's trying to
decide where the accent should

01:08:38.279 --> 01:08:42.420 align:middle line:84%
be, the single accent, it has
these various considerations.

01:08:42.420 --> 01:08:44.760 align:middle line:84%
It wants the accent to
be near the boundary.

01:08:44.760 --> 01:08:48.120 align:middle line:84%
It would like the accent
to be on the last word.

01:08:48.120 --> 01:08:50.939 align:middle line:84%
But more important than having
the accent on the last word

01:08:50.939 --> 01:08:53.819 align:middle line:84%
is avoiding having accent
on the last syllable.

01:08:53.819 --> 01:08:58.380 align:middle line:84%
So when it's deciding
between having

01:08:58.380 --> 01:09:00.149 align:middle line:84%
the accent on the
last syllable, which

01:09:00.149 --> 01:09:02.100 align:middle line:84%
would put the accent
on the last word,

01:09:02.100 --> 01:09:06.720 align:middle line:84%
and putting the accent at
the end of the first word

01:09:06.720 --> 01:09:08.984 align:middle line:84%
in the compound, it
chooses to put it

01:09:08.984 --> 01:09:10.859 align:middle line:84%
at the end of the first
word of the compound,

01:09:10.859 --> 01:09:13.420 align:middle line:84%
because, well, that puts
it near the boundary

01:09:13.420 --> 01:09:15.330 align:middle line:90%
and avoids having it be final.

01:09:15.330 --> 01:09:19.710 align:middle line:84%
So Japanese has these various
things it's trying to do

01:09:19.710 --> 01:09:22.270 align:middle line:84%
and it can't have
everything that it wants.

01:09:22.270 --> 01:09:24.785 align:middle line:84%
So it would like to
accent the second word.

01:09:24.785 --> 01:09:26.910 align:middle line:84%
It would like to put the
accent on the second word.

01:09:26.910 --> 01:09:29.368 align:middle line:84%
That's what we've seen when
the second word is long enough,

01:09:29.368 --> 01:09:31.270 align:middle line:84%
then the accent will
go on the second word.

01:09:31.270 --> 01:09:34.034 align:middle line:84%
But when the second
word is so short

01:09:34.034 --> 01:09:36.630 align:middle line:84%
that that would put it at
the end of the compound word,

01:09:36.630 --> 01:09:40.859 align:middle line:84%
then it doesn't go
on the second word.

01:09:40.859 --> 01:09:43.000 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't go at
the end of the word.

01:09:43.000 --> 01:09:47.279 align:middle line:84%
So avoiding having the
accent at the end of the word

01:09:47.279 --> 01:09:52.450 align:middle line:84%
is more important than putting
the accent on the last word.

01:09:52.450 --> 01:09:53.939 align:middle line:84%
So here's a place
where Japanese is

01:09:53.939 --> 01:09:57.150 align:middle line:84%
acting as though it has many
things that it wants and it

01:09:57.150 --> 01:09:59.640 align:middle line:90%
chooses the best one.

01:09:59.640 --> 01:10:01.920 align:middle line:84%
This is a very influential
way of thinking

01:10:01.920 --> 01:10:03.810 align:middle line:84%
about phonological
problems, which

01:10:03.810 --> 01:10:06.540 align:middle line:84%
we will talk about more next
time when we have more time.

01:10:06.540 --> 01:10:09.890 align:middle line:84%
Are there any questions about
it before I let you guys go?

01:10:09.890 --> 01:10:10.390 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

01:10:10.390 --> 01:10:11.860 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Does this
have a formal name?

01:10:11.860 --> 01:10:12.735 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes.

01:10:12.735 --> 01:10:14.852 align:middle line:84%
It is called Optimality
Theory, and we will

01:10:14.852 --> 01:10:16.060 align:middle line:90%
talk more about it next time.

01:10:16.060 --> 01:10:19.000 align:middle line:84%
You are seeking to make
your choices optimal,

01:10:19.000 --> 01:10:23.460 align:middle line:84%
to make them as
good as they can be.

01:10:23.460 --> 01:10:24.890 align:middle line:90%
Other questions?

01:10:24.890 --> 01:10:25.390 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:10:25.390 --> 01:10:28.750 align:middle line:84%
So again, I'll put up a
new problem set today,

01:10:28.750 --> 01:10:31.770 align:middle line:90%
and I'll see you guys next time.

01:10:31.770 --> 01:10:39.000 align:middle line:90%